WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:07.493
- I'm Steve Pitti.

00:00:07.493 --> 00:00:10.950
I'm a professor of ethnicity,
race and migration,

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.040
American Studies in histories,

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.690
and I'm director of Yale
Center for the study

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:17.610
of Race, Indigeneity and
Transnational Migration,

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:20.110
which has helped us support this event.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:22.720
The RITM Center at Yale exists

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.900
to help develop and draw
attention to urgent work

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.870
related to Race, Indigeneity
and Transnational Migration

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:31.100
and we're grateful for
our amazing colleagues

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.060
and collaborators, and indeed, all of you.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:38.260
Let me thank my professor,
Professor Lisa Lowe,

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:39.970
and the organizers of this event

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:41.300
for the gathering.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:42.710
Let me thank the panelists

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:44.690
and others who travel to be here.

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:46.370
And of course, our center staff,

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:48.300
including Nicole Edwards and Maggie Katz,

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.133
who've helped with many

00:00:49.133 --> 00:00:50.880
of the behind the scenes logistics

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.670
to make all this happen.

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:53.970
Here in this windowless space,

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:57.080
enclosed by cement, and steel, and wood

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:57.913
we're bubbled in

00:00:57.913 --> 00:00:59.703
the kind of architectural
membrane it seems to me

00:00:59.703 --> 00:01:01.820
that can too easily blind us

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.460
to where we actually are.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:05.900
So, I'll remind you that New Haven

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:07.663
has long been home

00:01:07.663 --> 00:01:12.090
to Mohegan Mashantucket
Pequot, Eastern Pequot,

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:15.600
Scattercoke, Golden Hill
Paugussett, Niantic,

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.500
Quinnipiac, and other
Algonquin speaking peoples.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.550
And then, we're surrounded
not just in this room,

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:23.012
but out in the rainy outdoors

00:01:23.012 --> 00:01:25.690
by patterns of violence,
but also, patterns

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:28.640
of creative survival and
persistence every day.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:30.330
For me, our location underscores

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:32.090
the importance of corroding

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:36.260
the seemingly impermeable
modernist walls here,

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:39.060
and the Gothic walls of
other university spaces.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:40.240
We've been looking forward to this event

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:41.240
for a long time.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:42.520
Thanks so much for coming.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:43.530
I'll now turn this over

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.552
to Elizabeth Hinton, Elizabeth?

00:01:45.552 --> 00:01:48.182
- [Male] Woo!
(audience applauds)

00:01:48.182 --> 00:01:50.030
- Thank you so much, Steve,

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:50.980
for that introduction.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.140
I'd also like to thank you
and the Center for the study

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:56.280
of Race, Indigeneity and
Transnational Migration

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.091
for all of your generous support

00:01:58.091 --> 00:01:59.850
for this event in particular,

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:02.000
to Nicole Edwards, Maggie Kats,

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.600
and the RITM staff for their work

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:07.560
in making arrangements for the visitors.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.690
I also have to give a huge thank you

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.938
to my colleague, Lisa Lowe.

00:02:11.938 --> 00:02:12.771
- [Male] Woo!

00:02:12.771 --> 00:02:14.171
- For really making this possible.

00:02:14.171 --> 00:02:16.130
(audience applauds)

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:19.140
And for doing just all the organizing

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:22.540
behind the scenes, and
thank all of you for coming.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:25.630
We have over 120 other people tuned

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:27.650
in virtually and counting.

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:28.970
So, I just really want to thank all

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:30.990
of you for joining us
for this conversation.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:31.823
I'm Elizabeth Hinton.

00:02:31.823 --> 00:02:34.500
I'm a professor of history
African American studies

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:35.840
and law here at Yale.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.630
I'm the author of "From the War on Poverty

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:38.910
to the War on Crime:

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:41.360
the Making of Mass
Incarceration in America,"

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:42.700
and "America on Fire:

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:45.040
the Untold History of Police Violence

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.197
and Black Rebellion Since the 1960s."

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:50.560
And it is such an honor

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.620
and a privilege to have the opportunity

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.940
to engage with Robin D.G. Kelley,

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:57.320
Derecka Purnell and Garrett Felber,

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:59.620
really among the best thinkers

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:02.720
and activists out there today.

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.030
To think collectively about liberation

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:07.660
and how we might create a better world.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:08.980
It's a conversation that

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:13.150
has increasingly grown more urgent,

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:14.640
even if some of the terms

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:18.640
and the contexts have changed
in the nearly two years

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:22.010
since the murders of Ahmaud
Aubrey, Breonna Taylor,

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:23.090
and George Floyd.

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:25.420
I think, we all really wanna know

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:26.480
where to go from here.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:29.170
So, we're gonna have a conversation

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.670
for about 15 minutes to an hour or so,

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:34.760
and then we'll open it up for questions.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.530
Those of you turning in virtually,

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:39.030
feel free to submit your
questions at any time.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:42.130
I'll be monitoring the chat or the Q&amp;A,

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:45.260
and maybe I'll throw a
couple in there during

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:47.470
the moderated conversation.

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:49.830
And then, when we open it up there,

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:52.040
you'll see two microphones on either side

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:52.990
of the stage.

00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:56.410
Please feel free to come
down and ask questions.

00:03:56.410 --> 00:03:58.950
That's really the most exciting part

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:00.770
of the conversation.

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:02.320
And then, after the event,

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:06.260
we encourage you to join
us for a book signing

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:08.740
with any of the authors up here,

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:10.480
but especially Derecka Purnell,

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.030
whose book I'll talk about in a second,

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:13.753
is the most recent.
(audience laughs)

00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:15.710
(audience cheers and claps)

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:17.260
He is excited about this, yeah.

00:04:18.292 --> 00:04:21.330
- [Derecka] Thank you.
- And that book signing

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:24.447
was made possible by
one of New Haven's gems,

00:04:24.447 --> 00:04:26.800
the independent bookstore,
People Get Ready Book.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:29.580
So, thank you so much to Lauren

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.090
and the People Get Ready team

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:32.550
for making this happen.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:34.040
All right, let me introduce

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:35.460
our esteemed panelists.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:38.870
I'll go in order of how they're seated.

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:42.380
So, Garrett Felber,
Garrett and I go way back

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:45.220
to grad school and we were both babies,

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:46.870
but he is...

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:48.030
He organized one

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:51.340
of the most important
conferences on abolition

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:53.430
that has taken place in recent years.

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:56.050
The making and unmaking
mass incarceration,

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:58.530
or MUMI Conference at the University

00:04:58.530 --> 00:04:59.960
of Mississippi, where he taught

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:03.239
in the history department until last year.

00:05:03.239 --> 00:05:06.690
MUMI, which took place in December, 2019

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:09.980
facilitated across
generational conversation

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:12.580
between students, scholars, and activists,

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:14.050
and it was really amazing.

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:16.010
Derecka and I were both there on a panel

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:17.940
on abolition, so we know.

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:20.330
And it was also (chuckles)
really important

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:22.157
because Garrett brought, Garrett held

00:05:22.157 --> 00:05:26.210
an unapologetically
abolitionist conference

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:28.070
in Mississippi.

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:31.330
Felber organizes many, many things,

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:34.820
but among those projects
is study and struggle,

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:36.890
which challenges criminalization

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:38.800
and incarceration in Mississippi

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:40.680
through mutual aid, political education,

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:42.410
and community building.

00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:45.120
Felber received his
PhD in American culture

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:46.640
from the University of Michigan

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:48.780
and is the author of
"Those Who Know Don't Say:

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:51.240
the Nation of Islam," "The
Black Freedom Movement

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.920
and the Carceral State," and the co-author

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:56.270
of "The Portable Malcolm X Reader

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:58.390
with the late Pulitzer
Prize winning historian,

00:05:58.390 --> 00:05:59.790
Manning Marable.

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:02.440
And Felber's currently with us at Yale

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:04.410
as a visiting fellow in American studies

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:05.880
and the Center for the study

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:08.730
of Race Indigeneity and
Transnational Migration,

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:11.200
where he has worked to
complete his next book,

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:13.210
a biography of former political prisoner,

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:14.397
Martin Sostre called,

00:06:14.397 --> 00:06:16.857
"We Are All Political Prisoners."

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:21.500
To Garrett's left is Derecka Purnell,

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:24.920
who is this generation's
most galvanizing voice

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:26.398
around abolition.
- Yeah.

00:06:26.398 --> 00:06:27.231
(audience laughs)

00:06:27.231 --> 00:06:28.064
(audience cheers)

00:06:28.064 --> 00:06:29.209
- [Elizabeth] Now, we all know it's true.

00:06:29.209 --> 00:06:31.179
(audience laughs and claps)
(male mumbles indistinctly)

00:06:31.179 --> 00:06:32.012
- Thank you.
(laughs)

00:06:32.012 --> 00:06:32.845
- [Elizabeth] She's a triple threat.

00:06:32.845 --> 00:06:34.100
She's a grassroots organizer,

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:36.850
she's a lawyer, and she's an author.

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:39.330
Her book, let me just show it all to you

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:40.867
so you will go purchase it,

00:06:40.867 --> 00:06:43.090
"Becoming Abolitionists Police Protest

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:45.070
in the Pursuit of Freedom,"

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:46.970
just came out last October.

00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:48.510
And once again, is available

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:49.520
for purchase outside.

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:51.000
You can get Derecka Purnell

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:52.230
to sign it for you today.

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:55.100
And in addition to this powerful book,

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:57.010
which everybody needs to read,

00:06:57.010 --> 00:06:57.843
and if you're in

00:06:57.843 --> 00:06:59.460
my African American history survey class,

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:00.810
you're gonna be reading it.

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:04.180
Purnell's writing has
been published widely,

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:05.450
including in the New York Times,

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:07.570
The Atlantic, The Boston
Review, Teen Vogue,

00:07:07.570 --> 00:07:09.650
and Harper's Bizarre.

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:12.650
Purnell received her JD
from Harvard Law School

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:13.970
and has lectured, studied

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:16.310
and strategized around
social movements across

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:17.143
the United States,

00:07:17.143 --> 00:07:18.852
the Netherlands, Belgium, South Africa,

00:07:18.852 --> 00:07:21.280
the United Kingdom, and Australia.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:22.820
She is currently a columnist at

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:24.840
The Guardian, a Margaret Burrows fellow

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:27.910
for the social justice
initiatives portal project

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:30.120
at the University of Illinois, Chicago.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.980
And a scholar in residence

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:35.530
at Columbia Law School.

00:07:35.530 --> 00:07:39.470
And finally, Robin D.G. Kelley

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:43.320
is the Gary B. Nash
Professor of history at UCLA

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:46.687
and the greatest living
historian in America.

00:07:46.687 --> 00:07:50.579
(audience claps and cheers)

00:07:50.579 --> 00:07:51.412
(Derecka laughs)

00:07:51.412 --> 00:07:52.245
He needs no introduction.

00:07:52.245 --> 00:07:53.078
So, I'm just gonna stop there.

00:07:53.078 --> 00:07:53.940
No, I'm just kidding.

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:57.700
If I were to properly
introduce Professor Kelley,

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:01.520
who I've known now for over 20 years,

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:02.580
I can't, 20 years.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:03.750
I'm aging us since I was

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:07.740
a bright eyed, 18 year old at NYU.

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:08.960
I would, I need to take
30 minutes before to get-

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:10.060
- Before that.

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:10.910
- Well, yeah, oh, yeah.

00:08:10.910 --> 00:08:12.510
- I went to your house
when you were a kid.

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:13.400
- Yes.

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:14.326
- 'Cause I know your dad.

00:08:14.326 --> 00:08:15.542
- But I don't remember you.

00:08:15.542 --> 00:08:17.102
(Professor Kelly laughs)

00:08:17.102 --> 00:08:18.050
(group laughs)
- Exactly.

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:19.470
- I just remember you from the intro

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:20.980
to black urban studies class.

00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:23.390
- Right, right.
(Derecka laughs)

00:08:23.390 --> 00:08:24.223
- But anyways, okay,

00:08:24.223 --> 00:08:26.755
so Professor Kelly received his PhD

00:08:26.755 --> 00:08:29.230
in history from UCLA and has been bringing

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:31.400
us hit after hit ever since

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:33.800
from his articles and
op-eds to his 13 books,

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:34.970
including the groundbreaking

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:36.350
and award-winning "Hammer

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:37.860
and Hoe Alabama Communist," since

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:39.700
the great depression, race, rebels,

00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:41.990
culture, politics in
the black working class.

00:08:41.990 --> 00:08:44.670
Freedom dreams, the
black radical imagination

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:46.957
and Thelonious Monk, the Life and Times

00:08:46.957 --> 00:08:49.970
of an American Original, among others.

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:52.500
And these works have illuminated aspects

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:54.030
of the history of social movements

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:56.260
and capitalist exploitation in the US

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:59.080
and the world, that have
inspired generations

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:01.260
of activists, organizers, and scholars

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:04.140
to struggle for racial
and economic justice.

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:06.220
So, getting to have this conversation

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:07.350
and sit down with the three of you

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:09.350
was really a dream come true for me

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:11.800
and I hope for all of you as well.

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:15.470
So, abolition is a process that

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:17.960
in many ways really begins
in our own minds, right?

00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:20.450
In our own understanding of the world

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:22.680
and our political and ethical commitments.

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:25.540
And all of you have been doing this work

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:27.960
for some time and so, I'm wondering

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:30.330
if you could each share with us

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:33.520
how you came to abolition as a politics

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:34.960
and as a practice.

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:38.930
And how you think about
the meaning of abolition,

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:42.013
I guess, how you define abolition.

00:09:43.130 --> 00:09:44.300
So, we'll start with Garrett, since he was

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:46.150
introduced first.
- Oh my God, okay.

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:49.130
Hi, everyone, can you hear me okay?

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:52.470
Well, it's such a...

00:09:52.470 --> 00:09:54.100
I don't know, I wiggled my way onto

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:55.410
this panel somehow,

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:56.569
and I'm just so grateful
(Robin laughs)

00:09:56.569 --> 00:09:57.669
(chuckles) to be here.

00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:01.490
And I wanna start by just saying thank you

00:10:01.490 --> 00:10:04.350
to all the incarcerated comrades,

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:08.260
who I struggle with and
learn from all the time.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:09.510
I'm hoping we can transcribe

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:11.550
this conversation and send it to them.

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:12.900
And then also, my co-panelists

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:15.810
who I've learned so much from.

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:18.300
So, most of anything I say helpful today

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:19.713
will come from those folks.

00:10:21.290 --> 00:10:22.640
I think, to begin with a definition

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:27.410
of abolition, for me, it's
a revolutionary practice

00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:31.640
to create an egalitarian society free

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:34.030
of coercion and domination.

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:36.200
That sort of obviates the need

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:38.863
for police, and prisons, and punishment.

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:43.670
And I emphasize, I think, both revolution

00:10:43.670 --> 00:10:46.620
and practice, because I think, one

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:47.730
of the mistakes that we make

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:49.510
is to think of abolition as a set

00:10:49.510 --> 00:10:54.020
of sort of beliefs, or an identity.

00:10:54.020 --> 00:10:57.550
And it really does us no good
to have people who identify-

00:10:57.550 --> 00:10:58.383
- [Elizabeth] Mm-hmm.

00:10:58.383 --> 00:10:59.350
- As abolitionists, right?

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:01.000
That doesn't get us free.

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:04.120
So, it's also not a destination.

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:09.080
We don't do things and arrive
at this place in particular.

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:11.440
So, I think it's something we do every day

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:13.270
and we stumble through it.

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:14.830
And I think, experimentation

00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:17.990
and creativity is sort of central for me,

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:19.173
to that practice.

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:23.363
Oh, my personal journey to it.

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:27.230
I really was sort of politicized

00:11:27.230 --> 00:11:30.170
through history, really, thanks

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:33.103
to Robin's writings in particular.

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:36.140
Freedom dreams, I mean, Derecka writes

00:11:36.140 --> 00:11:38.540
about this beautifully
in her book as well.

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:39.580
Freedom dreams for me

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:41.320
was a whole new roadmap to thinking

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:43.583
about radical social movements,

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:47.310
not in terms of success or failure,

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:51.330
but as a sort of collective
dream making process.

00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:53.230
And I read that the same year

00:11:53.230 --> 00:11:55.504
that I read "Black Study Black Struggle,"

00:11:55.504 --> 00:11:56.337
- [Derecka] Mm-hmm.

00:11:56.337 --> 00:11:59.730
- And that article, Robin talked

00:11:59.730 --> 00:12:04.330
about the classroom not
being a place of love,

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:06.870
and therefore, not a
place of deep critique.

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:08.410
And that really resonated with me

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:09.990
at the time because I was teaching

00:12:09.990 --> 00:12:11.330
in a minimum security prison.

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:14.600
We were teaching, learning about
the black radical tradition

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:16.963
and he mentioned study groups.

00:12:17.890 --> 00:12:19.810
And I was like, "What is a study group?"

00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:21.260
I've only, my encounter

00:12:21.260 --> 00:12:23.040
with these radical social movements

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:26.300
was only through university
and college spaces,

00:12:26.300 --> 00:12:27.610
which didn't feel like a place

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:30.770
of love, and deep critique,
and accountability.

00:12:30.770 --> 00:12:32.160
And I was for the first time

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:34.800
in a prison, encountering that space.

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:39.350
And that was a paradox,
or a contradiction to me,

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:41.270
like why was I encountering this space

00:12:41.270 --> 00:12:43.980
of love and commitment within such

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:47.810
a violent place?

00:12:47.810 --> 00:12:50.830
So, there was an undergraduate student

00:12:50.830 --> 00:12:52.510
at the time, who asked me my thoughts

00:12:52.510 --> 00:12:53.970
on abolition and I spouted off

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:56.040
some very uninformed things

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:59.520
about utopianism and it sounds nice,

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:00.790
but how would we ever do...

00:13:00.790 --> 00:13:03.450
Like the script that we
all sort of fall into

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:06.570
the first time I think,
we encounter abolition.

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:07.890
And I think, that script tells

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:09.660
us something really
important, a few things,

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:10.980
one of which is that we're all

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:13.100
sort of performing something

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:15.040
that we've been normalized to believe

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:18.780
about the function of prisons,
and punishment in society.

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:22.710
And it's actually very helpful
in organizing to undo them

00:13:22.710 --> 00:13:25.600
because we encounter
the same set of beliefs.

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:26.680
You know, what about the murders?

00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:28.200
What about the rapists?

00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:30.800
It's utopian, how would we
ever create this society?

00:13:32.830 --> 00:13:34.410
So, I guess, it didn't take me that long

00:13:34.410 --> 00:13:38.040
to sort of get from that place

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:40.650
to believing that prisons shouldn't exist,

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:42.050
and police should not exist.

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:44.800
But I really think the practice

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:47.830
of what that actually looks like beyond

00:13:47.830 --> 00:13:49.560
just sort of going around pronouncing

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:50.895
that I believe this thing

00:13:50.895 --> 00:13:54.160
is the ongoing work of abolition.

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:56.294
Of every day being in touch

00:13:56.294 --> 00:13:57.910
with people inside, figuring out

00:13:57.910 --> 00:14:02.910
how to support them and
to dream collectively.

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:05.540
I can maybe talk about
that more throughout,

00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:08.970
but I think, so much of the way for me,

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:10.710
the journey through this has been always

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:12.455
in sort of tension and tandem

00:14:12.455 --> 00:14:15.120
between the prison and the university.

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:17.110
And it's been really helpful
for me to understand the way

00:14:17.110 --> 00:14:19.810
that both of those institutions work

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:23.720
to stop collective dreaming.

00:14:23.720 --> 00:14:25.170
Those are institutions that work

00:14:25.170 --> 00:14:29.540
to silo us to venerate individual thought

00:14:29.540 --> 00:14:32.590
and solitary thought, and
not collective thought

00:14:32.590 --> 00:14:34.003
and collective mobilization.

00:14:35.836 --> 00:14:37.860
So, for me, that's been

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:39.610
the sort of ongoing work is to figure out

00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:44.610
how to collectively dream and understand

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:46.803
the people who

00:14:46.803 --> 00:14:48.240
have collectively dreamed before us.

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:49.073
- [Robin] Mm.

00:14:49.073 --> 00:14:50.080
- Because we're so often cut off

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:51.500
from all the work that they've done

00:14:51.500 --> 00:14:52.700
and that's why I think, freedom dreams

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:53.860
for me was such a touchstone,

00:14:53.860 --> 00:14:57.260
because I realized that
we have to understand

00:14:57.260 --> 00:15:00.014
that ongoing process
of collective dreaming.

00:15:00.014 --> 00:15:02.348
(door creaks open)

00:15:02.348 --> 00:15:03.480
(panelists chatter indistinctly)

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:04.470
Oh, I have a couple of clips

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:05.540
of comrades inside the play,

00:15:05.540 --> 00:15:07.026
but maybe I'll do it after both of you go,

00:15:07.026 --> 00:15:07.993
'cause I don't wanna-

00:15:07.993 --> 00:15:08.826
(crosstalk drowns out speaker)

00:15:08.826 --> 00:15:09.659
- Oh, you can play it now.

00:15:09.659 --> 00:15:10.742
(crosstalk drowns out speaker)
- Not like the last time.

00:15:10.742 --> 00:15:15.742
(crosstalk drowns out speaker)
Okay, all, right.

00:15:16.755 --> 00:15:17.717
I don't know if this works,

00:15:17.717 --> 00:15:18.610
can people just hear me if I shout?

00:15:18.610 --> 00:15:21.887
Okay, so one of the
things when I was trying

00:15:21.887 --> 00:15:24.310
to think of something useful to say today

00:15:24.310 --> 00:15:26.590
was that I just wanted,

00:15:26.590 --> 00:15:28.970
really people to hear from folks inside,

00:15:28.970 --> 00:15:29.803
who I organized with.

00:15:29.803 --> 00:15:32.420
So, I'm just gonna play a couple of clips.

00:15:32.420 --> 00:15:34.550
I asked them to share
a few words with you.

00:15:34.550 --> 00:15:37.070
The first one is from

00:15:37.070 --> 00:15:38.410
a dear comrade, Lawrence Jenkins,

00:15:38.410 --> 00:15:40.540
he's incarcerated in Washington State.

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:45.540
Just a revolutionary,
organizer, farmer, artist.

00:15:46.890 --> 00:15:48.800
And the second and Stevie Wilson,

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:51.050
who folks might be familiar with as well,

00:15:51.050 --> 00:15:52.550
who's incarcerated in Pennsylvania.

00:15:52.550 --> 00:15:53.850
So, I'll just share those.

00:15:56.490 --> 00:15:57.640
- [Lawrence] I wanna
speak on the importance

00:15:57.640 --> 00:15:59.880
of political education and solidarity

00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:01.990
in our struggle to survive, get free,

00:16:01.990 --> 00:16:03.150
and stay free.

00:16:03.150 --> 00:16:04.570
But first, I would like to address

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:05.403
the shortcomings of

00:16:05.403 --> 00:16:08.240
the prison abolition movement today.

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:09.527
There is a belief that future aid

00:16:09.527 --> 00:16:11.690
and general (indistinct) respondents alone

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:14.450
will help us survive
genocide and escape torture,

00:16:14.450 --> 00:16:16.790
when instead, these caring acts only help

00:16:16.790 --> 00:16:19.475
us manage and cope with these conditions

00:16:19.475 --> 00:16:21.460
while doing nothing to
liberate us from them.

00:16:21.460 --> 00:16:23.120
This approach will only help prisoners

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:26.830
suffer peacefully and while assimilating

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:28.970
our movement into the power establishment,

00:16:28.970 --> 00:16:29.803
it was control

00:16:29.803 --> 00:16:32.470
of (indistinct) state
violence and depression.

00:16:32.470 --> 00:16:34.279
The very thing that developed (indistinct)

00:16:34.279 --> 00:16:36.790
as a tool to displace and dispose

00:16:36.790 --> 00:16:38.360
an entire community.

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:40.520
This must be met with critical resistance

00:16:40.520 --> 00:16:42.573
and revolutionary action.

00:16:43.718 --> 00:16:45.400
It is on us abolitionists

00:16:45.400 --> 00:16:46.660
for sincerely committed,

00:16:46.660 --> 00:16:48.560
not just to thought in words,

00:16:48.560 --> 00:16:50.970
but in action and practical application.

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:52.890
We are confronting material conditions

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:55.380
that must be done away with completely.

00:16:55.380 --> 00:16:57.300
We are embracing visions, programs,

00:16:57.300 --> 00:17:00.870
and processes that ensure
autonomy, independence,

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:03.730
collective self-determination
and sustainability.

00:17:03.730 --> 00:17:05.370
These are things we've identified

00:17:05.370 --> 00:17:07.670
as primary aims and objectives

00:17:07.670 --> 00:17:10.140
to ensure our survival,
maintain our freedom,

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:11.600
and secure our destiny.

00:17:11.600 --> 00:17:15.170
Now, we are committed to
do this until they free us.

00:17:15.170 --> 00:17:17.750
Being that we were
having this conversation

00:17:17.750 --> 00:17:19.760
in a higher (indistinct)
institution today,

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:21.990
I must emphasize importance

00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:25.210
of political education, as
well as cultural studies

00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:27.000
and why it's needed for prisoners.

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:29.080
Simply put, we lost the knowledge

00:17:29.080 --> 00:17:30.560
of what we once knew.

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:32.773
We lost control of what we once want.

00:17:34.660 --> 00:17:37.770
We lost the belief that we can do whatever

00:17:37.770 --> 00:17:39.080
we put our mind to.

00:17:39.080 --> 00:17:41.790
We need this reeducation
and re-empowerment

00:17:41.790 --> 00:17:43.060
in order to further develop

00:17:43.060 --> 00:17:45.020
a counterculture that can be shared

00:17:45.020 --> 00:17:47.337
with the mass movement
fighting on our behalf,

00:17:47.337 --> 00:17:49.440
and on behalf of our
loved ones in community

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:53.430
against mass imprisonment,
racism, and oppression.

00:17:53.430 --> 00:17:56.120
We are all at different
stages of development

00:17:56.120 --> 00:17:57.130
in our journey.

00:17:57.130 --> 00:18:00.550
Each state has its own
laws, policy and practices.

00:18:00.550 --> 00:18:02.350
Even prison has its own variation

00:18:02.350 --> 00:18:05.940
of genocide, slavery, and
cruel, identified torture,

00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:08.080
but that's leading us where we are at.

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:10.010
Coming with a sincere commitment

00:18:10.010 --> 00:18:11.550
to helping us over...

00:18:11.550 --> 00:18:13.500
To helping us come to a level

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:15.820
of consciousness and
ability that will help

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:18.030
us not only meet our basic needs,

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:20.780
but also help us achieve
our (indistinct) objectives

00:18:20.780 --> 00:18:22.140
and secure our freedom.

00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:23.510
And Grace Lee Boggs taught us

00:18:23.510 --> 00:18:26.200
about revolutionary humanism, organization

00:18:26.200 --> 00:18:28.900
means commitment, and that's what we need

00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:31.513
from today, the abolitionists over.

00:18:32.519 --> 00:18:34.410
