00:32:58.740 --> 00:33:00.438
And I thought that was BS.

00:33:00.438 --> 00:33:01.271
(audience laughs)

00:33:01.271 --> 00:33:02.760
I wanted Robin to give
me like, eight books.

00:33:02.760 --> 00:33:05.620
I wanted a political education starter kit

00:33:05.620 --> 00:33:07.100
and he was not about that life.

00:33:07.100 --> 00:33:08.491
I was very frustrated about it.

00:33:08.491 --> 00:33:09.324
(Robin laughs)

00:33:09.324 --> 00:33:11.280
And then, I went to South
Africa and I watched,

00:33:11.280 --> 00:33:12.120
I watched that happen.

00:33:12.120 --> 00:33:13.910
I watched students read

00:33:13.910 --> 00:33:17.190
and debate different
political texts, and how...

00:33:17.190 --> 00:33:19.794
And they used that to inform the demands

00:33:19.794 --> 00:33:22.160
that they needed in order to fight

00:33:22.160 --> 00:33:23.950
for the freedoms that they wanted.

00:33:23.950 --> 00:33:25.190
So, I was there.

00:33:25.190 --> 00:33:26.590
I was watching students say,

00:33:27.617 --> 00:33:29.720
"Our movement is about free education

00:33:29.720 --> 00:33:31.100
because we think that education

00:33:31.100 --> 00:33:32.540
is a tool of empowerment,"

00:33:32.540 --> 00:33:34.950
similar to what Lawrence
just said on the call.

00:33:34.950 --> 00:33:37.630
And we believe that it
will help facilitate

00:33:37.630 --> 00:33:39.687
our fight for the land here.

00:33:39.687 --> 00:33:42.290
And so, they were making these
broader connections between

00:33:42.290 --> 00:33:43.713
their educational struggles,

00:33:44.695 --> 00:33:47.010
and these broader decolonial demands

00:33:47.010 --> 00:33:50.410
that to help transform the
world that they belong to.

00:33:50.410 --> 00:33:51.670
And I said this the other day,

00:33:51.670 --> 00:33:55.230
I was like, "Well, I was
there watching them read

00:33:55.230 --> 00:33:57.870
and debate, sometimes
physically fight over

00:33:57.870 --> 00:33:59.168
their ideas, 'cause it...

00:33:59.168 --> 00:34:02.610
They knew that there was so much at stake.

00:34:02.610 --> 00:34:05.410
And I remember coming back to
the west and thinking about

00:34:05.410 --> 00:34:07.530
our student demands that weren't informed

00:34:07.530 --> 00:34:09.120
about political education.

00:34:09.120 --> 00:34:11.330
And I was like, they're
trying to get the land.

00:34:11.330 --> 00:34:13.276
we're trying to get a diversity office.

00:34:13.276 --> 00:34:16.070
(audience laughs)
Like it was not the same.

00:34:16.070 --> 00:34:18.155
This is very, very different.

00:34:18.155 --> 00:34:19.830
(audience laughs)
Very, very, very different,

00:34:19.830 --> 00:34:21.720
but that showed me how important

00:34:21.720 --> 00:34:24.700
a political education man, how important,

00:34:24.700 --> 00:34:26.610
how important it was to be a part

00:34:26.610 --> 00:34:29.510
of a political home and
a political organization.

00:34:29.510 --> 00:34:32.850
To help spur your own
individual development,

00:34:32.850 --> 00:34:34.647
but to also do that with other people

00:34:34.647 --> 00:34:37.530
and you guys are growing and figuring out

00:34:37.530 --> 00:34:40.630
how to waste these
fights for the long term.

00:34:40.630 --> 00:34:42.120
And it doesn't directly answer

00:34:42.120 --> 00:34:44.090
my journey to become an abolitionist,

00:34:44.090 --> 00:34:45.840
'cause it wasn't linear, right?

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:48.918
It happened, a lot of
people become politicized

00:34:48.918 --> 00:34:49.751
in social movements.

00:34:49.751 --> 00:34:52.080
You sort of hear people talking

00:34:52.080 --> 00:34:55.380
about different ideas, abolished borders,

00:34:55.380 --> 00:34:57.140
or more diversity.

00:34:57.140 --> 00:35:00.380
And you can't quite make sense of them.

00:35:00.380 --> 00:35:02.910
You know that there is a broader history

00:35:02.910 --> 00:35:04.470
of people who fight for racial,

00:35:04.470 --> 00:35:06.010
and freedom, and justice.

00:35:06.010 --> 00:35:07.690
But until we start understanding

00:35:07.690 --> 00:35:09.680
the role of political education,

00:35:09.680 --> 00:35:12.110
we don't understand that history, right,

00:35:12.110 --> 00:35:14.670
of people who are on the good side,

00:35:14.670 --> 00:35:17.083
it's very different from
something like tradition.

00:35:17.083 --> 00:35:18.700
- [Robin] Mm-hmm.
- Where people fight

00:35:18.700 --> 00:35:20.550
for different things according

00:35:20.550 --> 00:35:23.240
to a set of belief that they think

00:35:23.240 --> 00:35:25.227
will help propel them
towards something greater.

00:35:25.227 --> 00:35:27.370
And I was being politicized around

00:35:27.370 --> 00:35:28.889
these different traditions.

00:35:28.889 --> 00:35:31.080
Nile is gonna kill me, but I was telling

00:35:31.080 --> 00:35:33.150
his story literally the other day too,

00:35:33.150 --> 00:35:35.590
about at his parents house.

00:35:35.590 --> 00:35:37.450
There's a picture on a wall

00:35:37.450 --> 00:35:41.300
and it's like Barack
Obama and Tupac Shakur,

00:35:41.300 --> 00:35:44.440
and Harriet Tubman, and Fannie Lou Hamer,

00:35:44.440 --> 00:35:48.190
and all these amazing
black figures in history.

00:35:48.190 --> 00:35:51.130
Like riding on horses to freedom, right?

00:35:51.130 --> 00:35:53.870
And I remember years ago I would've seen

00:35:53.870 --> 00:35:55.567
a picture like that and been like,

00:35:55.567 --> 00:35:57.450
"Oh, this is so amazing.

00:35:57.450 --> 00:35:59.230
Look at our history, right?"

00:35:59.230 --> 00:36:02.900
And then, you develop some
political analysis, right?

00:36:02.900 --> 00:36:05.980
Especially radical leftist
political analysis.

00:36:05.980 --> 00:36:08.190
And you realize that these are all very,

00:36:08.190 --> 00:36:10.560
very different people that we group

00:36:10.560 --> 00:36:13.110
in together and say,
"Look at our history."

00:36:13.110 --> 00:36:15.670
All right, Kamala Harris
and Fannie Lou Hamer,

00:36:15.670 --> 00:36:17.012
very, very different.

00:36:17.012 --> 00:36:17.845
(audience laughs)

00:36:17.845 --> 00:36:19.310
Very, very, very different, you know?

00:36:19.310 --> 00:36:20.343
Would not recommend one,

00:36:20.343 --> 00:36:22.960
they're very, very, very different, right?

00:36:22.960 --> 00:36:24.920
And so, but we would just loop them in,

00:36:24.920 --> 00:36:27.020
when we group them
together as black people

00:36:27.020 --> 00:36:29.220
and black women who are trying to fight

00:36:29.220 --> 00:36:30.620
for that seat at the table.

00:36:30.620 --> 00:36:33.210
We miss what makes them possible.

00:36:33.210 --> 00:36:35.910
And so, I was being pushed around,

00:36:35.910 --> 00:36:37.870
what are these different institutions?

00:36:37.870 --> 00:36:40.960
Like policing, like
prisons, like capitalism,

00:36:40.960 --> 00:36:43.860
like all these things I
had taken for granted,

00:36:43.860 --> 00:36:44.693
even though I had different

00:36:44.693 --> 00:36:46.020
touchpoints with them.

00:36:46.020 --> 00:36:47.700
'Cause I had been student organizer since

00:36:47.700 --> 00:36:50.100
I was about 14, trying to make sense

00:36:50.100 --> 00:36:51.200
of them alone.

00:36:51.200 --> 00:36:53.620
And then, once I realized, "Okay,

00:36:53.620 --> 00:36:54.657
I have to go through this

00:36:54.657 --> 00:36:56.770
and understand this with other people."

00:36:56.770 --> 00:36:59.850
That's why I started developing
more radical politics.

00:36:59.850 --> 00:37:01.650
Simply because I was surrounded by people

00:37:01.650 --> 00:37:03.230
who were more curious than I was,

00:37:03.230 --> 00:37:04.640
who were more courageous than I was,

00:37:04.640 --> 00:37:06.160
who were more radical than I was.

00:37:06.160 --> 00:37:08.852
And who were honestly patient,

00:37:08.852 --> 00:37:11.050
who weren't trying to convert me

00:37:11.050 --> 00:37:14.240
to do something, to
believe something, right?

00:37:14.240 --> 00:37:16.220
Who were actually trying to invite me

00:37:16.220 --> 00:37:18.240
to understand a set of radical politics

00:37:18.240 --> 00:37:20.140
that then, we can force together.

00:37:20.140 --> 00:37:22.310
So, that's why I really hope
the spirit of the book is,

00:37:22.310 --> 00:37:25.210
is actually less about this is where I was

00:37:25.210 --> 00:37:26.500
and now, this is where I am.

00:37:26.500 --> 00:37:28.860
But actually, all of these touchpoints

00:37:28.860 --> 00:37:29.693
for different people

00:37:29.693 --> 00:37:31.756
I encounter, other
organizers, other movements,

00:37:31.756 --> 00:37:34.410
other ideas that help me understand

00:37:34.410 --> 00:37:37.160
that the invitation to
understand radical politics

00:37:37.160 --> 00:37:38.790
is much, much, much more

00:37:38.790 --> 00:37:42.090
than we identify as an
individual abolitionist.

00:37:42.090 --> 00:37:44.380
Which is why the title is
plural and not singular.

00:37:44.380 --> 00:37:45.213
- [Robin] Right.
- 'Cause it's not just

00:37:45.213 --> 00:37:46.810
about my individual story,

00:37:46.810 --> 00:37:49.070
but this generation who really

00:37:49.070 --> 00:37:50.507
hasn't been in conversation with people

00:37:50.507 --> 00:37:52.480
in the other parts of the world,

00:37:52.480 --> 00:37:55.878
and people across time, and
figured out how to get free.

00:37:55.878 --> 00:37:57.814
- [Robin] Right, mm.

00:37:57.814 --> 00:38:00.814
(audience applauds)

00:38:04.893 --> 00:38:06.370
- That is so true.

00:38:06.370 --> 00:38:07.640
And you know, you really,

00:38:07.640 --> 00:38:10.142
if you don't have the book, shame on you.

00:38:10.142 --> 00:38:11.082
(Derecka and audience chuckle)

00:38:11.082 --> 00:38:11.915
You really need to get it

00:38:11.915 --> 00:38:16.915
because in some ways it is both exactly

00:38:17.040 --> 00:38:22.000
what Derecka said in
terms of movement history,

00:38:22.000 --> 00:38:24.940
but it also is about the way concepts

00:38:24.940 --> 00:38:27.134
like abolition change over time

00:38:27.134 --> 00:38:28.860
in struggle.
- Mm-hmm.

00:38:28.860 --> 00:38:31.090
- It doesn't change because
a think tank is better

00:38:31.090 --> 00:38:32.470
than another think tank,

00:38:32.470 --> 00:38:33.850
it's the movements themselves

00:38:33.850 --> 00:38:35.750
produce certain kinds of (mumbles).

00:38:35.750 --> 00:38:39.190
So, let me... Let me go,
I took all these notes.

00:38:39.190 --> 00:38:42.180
I love the image, this
image of the picture.

00:38:42.180 --> 00:38:43.370
See, my generation.

00:38:43.370 --> 00:38:44.203
- Yes.

00:38:44.203 --> 00:38:45.430
- The pictures that you had on the wall

00:38:45.430 --> 00:38:49.705
were like kings of Africa,
a Budweiser, right?

00:38:49.705 --> 00:38:51.273
(Derecka and audience laugh)

00:38:51.273 --> 00:38:53.630
And you talk about being traumatized,

00:38:53.630 --> 00:38:56.463
like kings of Africa,
that was like our heroes.

00:38:57.930 --> 00:38:59.290
It wasn't like kings

00:38:59.290 --> 00:39:01.160
of Africa anti-imperialist kings.

00:39:01.160 --> 00:39:02.220
- No, Mansa Musa, like that kind of-

00:39:02.220 --> 00:39:04.810
- Right, exactly.
- That kind of energy.

00:39:04.810 --> 00:39:08.160
- And also, I just
wanna remind people that

00:39:08.160 --> 00:39:10.300
that piece, Black Study Black Struggle

00:39:10.300 --> 00:39:13.540
is a forum, it's not juts my essay.

00:39:13.540 --> 00:39:17.660
And in the forum is a really
brilliant contribution

00:39:17.660 --> 00:39:19.592
by Derecka Purnell.

00:39:19.592 --> 00:39:21.730
So, you need to read the whole forum,

00:39:21.730 --> 00:39:25.052
even the people who talk
really bad about me.

00:39:25.052 --> 00:39:26.325
(group chuckles)

00:39:26.325 --> 00:39:27.158
But it's really worth checking out.

00:39:27.158 --> 00:39:30.200
And it was a really important
kind of intervention.

00:39:30.200 --> 00:39:32.870
I really have, before I
sort of answer the question,

00:39:32.870 --> 00:39:34.690
I just have to acknowledge

00:39:34.690 --> 00:39:37.970
how important it is to be
with these three people.

00:39:37.970 --> 00:39:41.880
Specifically because I'm the elder,

00:39:44.170 --> 00:39:46.600
I know that, but I'm also the student

00:39:46.600 --> 00:39:47.623
of all three.

00:39:49.504 --> 00:39:53.050
And if you want, if you have to...

00:39:53.050 --> 00:39:53.910
I don't wanna give lists,

00:39:53.910 --> 00:39:55.760
but if you have to read three books

00:39:56.692 --> 00:39:59.030
that has to do with the struggle

00:39:59.030 --> 00:40:02.960
to try to end the carceral state

00:40:02.960 --> 00:40:04.453
in state violence,

00:40:05.790 --> 00:40:10.310
you read Garrett, Liz and Derecka's book.

00:40:10.310 --> 00:40:14.683
Those, and of course, both of Liz's books.

00:40:16.081 --> 00:40:18.410
These are really essential texts,

00:40:18.410 --> 00:40:21.460
and there's nothing like having Liz Hinton

00:40:21.460 --> 00:40:23.190
as my student, because she was also

00:40:23.190 --> 00:40:25.733
my research assistant, among other things.

00:40:26.620 --> 00:40:30.580
But I take a lot of notes.

00:40:30.580 --> 00:40:31.840
I learned so much from her.

00:40:31.840 --> 00:40:33.885
I learned so much from Garrett.

00:40:33.885 --> 00:40:37.530
When Garrett was working
on the Malcolm X project,

00:40:37.530 --> 00:40:38.695
and I was like...

00:40:38.695 --> 00:40:39.903
And of course, Derecka.

00:40:39.903 --> 00:40:42.307
So, I'm a student of these three people,

00:40:42.307 --> 00:40:44.313
and I'm a student of many, many people.

00:40:46.180 --> 00:40:48.520
When I think about my own journey,

00:40:48.520 --> 00:40:50.720
I think about the folks I learned

00:40:50.720 --> 00:40:52.182
from over the years.

00:40:52.182 --> 00:40:54.232
I'm talking early years, but later years.

00:40:55.582 --> 00:40:56.980
I'm a student of Eliza Kelly.

00:40:56.980 --> 00:40:58.580
I'm a student of Sherry Randolph,

00:40:58.580 --> 00:40:59.413
who's over there.

00:40:59.413 --> 00:41:01.050
I mean, Sherry was a student of mine,

00:41:01.050 --> 00:41:02.863
but I was really a student of hers.

00:41:03.700 --> 00:41:05.350
Michael Denning remembers those days

00:41:05.350 --> 00:41:08.000
when a handful of us
would get on the train

00:41:08.000 --> 00:41:09.504
to come to New Haven,

00:41:09.504 --> 00:41:12.930
to participate with
all the faculty at Yale

00:41:12.930 --> 00:41:14.330
to support the Union.

00:41:14.330 --> 00:41:15.889
What was it four people?

00:41:15.889 --> 00:41:19.091
It was like you and
Hazel, David Montgomery.

00:41:19.091 --> 00:41:19.924
(audience laughs)

00:41:19.924 --> 00:41:23.463
So, it's like I learned
from Michael over the years.

00:41:23.463 --> 00:41:24.910
So, I'm very fortunate.

00:41:24.910 --> 00:41:25.743
I'm just a student.

00:41:25.743 --> 00:41:29.170
So, having said that, I'm not gonna answer

00:41:29.170 --> 00:41:31.113
the question on what is abolition,

00:41:31.950 --> 00:41:35.430
because I think, both the brilliant answer

00:41:35.430 --> 00:41:38.480
that Garrett gave and everything

00:41:38.480 --> 00:41:41.100
that Derecka said, especially in her book;

00:41:41.100 --> 00:41:42.187
answers that question for me.

00:41:42.187 --> 00:41:43.860
But I'll say some things about my journey,

00:41:43.860 --> 00:41:46.240
'cause it helps to understand

00:41:46.240 --> 00:41:50.137
how I came to understand abolition.

00:41:50.137 --> 00:41:52.743
And so, first of all, I
have to begin by saying,

00:41:55.570 --> 00:41:57.147
back in the 1980s,

00:41:59.160 --> 00:42:01.363
no one called themselves an abolitionist.

00:42:02.640 --> 00:42:04.880
To say that you're for abolition

00:42:04.880 --> 00:42:06.590
was a very specific thing.

00:42:06.590 --> 00:42:09.650
It was like you're for abolishing prisons

00:42:09.650 --> 00:42:11.533
and abolishing the police.

00:42:12.668 --> 00:42:14.363
And so, it wasn't an identity.

00:42:15.290 --> 00:42:16.123
So, I come...

00:42:16.123 --> 00:42:18.987
So, at 18 years old,
I'm a communist, okay?

00:42:18.987 --> 00:42:20.253
And very proud of it.

00:42:22.170 --> 00:42:23.003
- [Audience Member] Woo hoo.

00:42:23.003 --> 00:42:23.836
(Derecka chuckles)

00:42:23.836 --> 00:42:26.500
And so, when you're a
communist and you're 18,

00:42:26.500 --> 00:42:28.480
you already have a view of the world,

00:42:28.480 --> 00:42:30.082
what it could be.

00:42:30.082 --> 00:42:32.780
And that view is things
that we think of as anti,

00:42:32.780 --> 00:42:34.373
we call it anti capitalist now.

00:42:35.250 --> 00:42:37.799
We didn't say anti-capitalist,
we just assumed, (laughs)

00:42:37.799 --> 00:42:39.320
it was anti capitalist.
- [Derecka] Yeah.

00:42:39.320 --> 00:42:42.270
- The destruction of capitalism itself.

00:42:42.270 --> 00:42:43.670
Seizing... Well of course, we had

00:42:43.670 --> 00:42:46.280
a state centered, state oriented idea.

00:42:46.280 --> 00:42:47.160
So, you seize the state,

00:42:47.160 --> 00:42:48.390
but the most important things

00:42:48.390 --> 00:42:53.341
that things like a
transformed environment,

00:42:53.341 --> 00:42:58.341
a fair wage, housing, transportation,

00:42:58.580 --> 00:43:02.380
ultimately the elimination of commodities,

00:43:02.380 --> 00:43:04.760
these are the things that we dreamed of.

00:43:04.760 --> 00:43:07.560
What's interesting about being a communist

00:43:07.560 --> 00:43:12.560
in the 19, basically,
1980, really, to be honest.

00:43:13.300 --> 00:43:18.300
80, 81, is that we didn't always think

00:43:18.570 --> 00:43:21.623
of the prison, at least not my circles,

00:43:22.830 --> 00:43:26.520
as the thing that needs to be eliminated.

00:43:26.520 --> 00:43:28.220
What we did do though,

00:43:28.220 --> 00:43:31.295
is that we had a sense of
what a political prisoner was.

00:43:31.295 --> 00:43:32.128
- [Derecka] Mm-hmm.

00:43:32.128 --> 00:43:33.561
- And this goes back to the title

00:43:33.561 --> 00:43:37.077
of Garrett's book on Martin Sostre.

00:43:39.580 --> 00:43:43.890
We didn't realize that they're
all political prisoners.

00:43:43.890 --> 00:43:45.820
Of course, that idea is emerging.

00:43:45.820 --> 00:43:49.400
In 1976, you have a... You
have development of documents

00:43:49.400 --> 00:43:51.530
that are arguing for
the abolition of prison.

00:43:51.530 --> 00:43:55.020
So, I grew up in the generation

00:43:55.020 --> 00:43:57.980
that said free Jeronimo Ji Jaga Pratt,

00:43:57.980 --> 00:43:59.330
free Sandiago (indistinct).

00:44:00.710 --> 00:44:02.453
Assata Shakur broke out,

00:44:03.610 --> 00:44:06.050
but there was a free Assata
Shakur at some point.

00:44:06.050 --> 00:44:08.490
So, '70s into the 1980s,

00:44:08.490 --> 00:44:11.000
there was a whole question
about political prisoners

00:44:11.000 --> 00:44:12.700
as a separate category.
- [Derecka] Mm-hmm.

00:44:12.700 --> 00:44:15.263
- Now, so here's my development.

00:44:17.340 --> 00:44:22.340
Number one, in 1988, I'm a
brand new PhD just finished.

00:44:24.270 --> 00:44:29.270
I meet Ruth Wilson Gilmore,
who becomes a lifelong friend.

00:44:29.870 --> 00:44:32.090
She was on her way to graduate school.

00:44:32.090 --> 00:44:35.500
She actually, she's a
Yale alum in theater,

00:44:35.500 --> 00:44:37.237
you know that, right?

00:44:37.237 --> 00:44:38.070
Do you know that?

00:44:38.070 --> 00:44:39.736
Oh, you know that.
(audience chuckles)

00:44:39.736 --> 00:44:41.840
But, so she was on her
way to go to Rutgers,

00:44:41.840 --> 00:44:44.750
to do a PhD in geography.

00:44:44.750 --> 00:44:48.253
And she had some ideas that blew my mind,

00:44:49.550 --> 00:44:52.440
and we weren't talking about abolition,

00:44:52.440 --> 00:44:55.643
but she was talking about the
relationship between space,

00:44:56.810 --> 00:45:00.270
prison construction, and all the violence

00:45:00.270 --> 00:45:03.873
that happens to the land, to
the environment, to workers,

00:45:05.476 --> 00:45:08.190
and to human beings put in cages.

00:45:08.190 --> 00:45:11.230
And she's trying to
figure that out spatially.

00:45:11.230 --> 00:45:12.870
And so, we had those early conversations

00:45:12.870 --> 00:45:15.110
are like, wow, that's amazing.

00:45:15.110 --> 00:45:17.870
So, of course, we
maintain lifelong friends.

00:45:17.870 --> 00:45:20.800
I take my second job, which is a job

00:45:20.800 --> 00:45:22.140
at the University of Michigan.

00:45:22.140 --> 00:45:23.960
And so, we have a Michigan connection,

00:45:23.960 --> 00:45:26.100
Liz Garrett, me.

00:45:26.100 --> 00:45:29.900
And they know, so I was there 1991

00:45:29.900 --> 00:45:31.940
to about 1994.

00:45:31.940 --> 00:45:33.623
Eliza was a little baby.

00:45:34.710 --> 00:45:37.483
And so, well, I'm there and
I end up doing two things

00:45:37.483 --> 00:45:38.730
that are significant.

00:45:38.730 --> 00:45:43.730
One, I teach at a place
called Boysville, 1992.

00:45:44.030 --> 00:45:49.030
Boysville is a place where they
detain kids, male children.

00:45:52.943 --> 00:45:54.390
This is the carceral state,

00:45:54.390 --> 00:45:56.490
this is the juvenile detention center.

00:45:56.490 --> 00:46:01.207
And I go in there and I'm
teaching, and all I see are poets.

00:46:05.000 --> 00:46:08.770
That's all I see, a
whole room full of poets,

00:46:08.770 --> 00:46:10.774
beautiful children,

00:46:10.774 --> 00:46:14.150
they're all black and
the couple Latin X kids.

00:46:14.150 --> 00:46:18.050
But black kids, or poets, and I'm like,

00:46:18.050 --> 00:46:19.363
and you're in here.

00:46:20.460 --> 00:46:22.180
And they made me poetry.

00:46:22.180 --> 00:46:23.310
I still have that poetry.

00:46:23.310 --> 00:46:27.607
They made laminated poems, right?

00:46:27.607 --> 00:46:30.010
And then, while I'm
there, Michigan, teaching

00:46:30.010 --> 00:46:31.280
at the University of Michigan,

00:46:31.280 --> 00:46:33.430
I had an opportunity
to go to Jackson State

00:46:34.550 --> 00:46:36.910
and meet some of the older brothers there.

00:46:36.910 --> 00:46:39.310
And what do I find?

00:46:39.310 --> 00:46:43.820
Poets, everywhere you turn, poets,

00:46:43.820 --> 00:46:46.610
and they're intellectuals,
and they're writers,

00:46:46.610 --> 00:46:47.960
and they're scholars.

00:46:47.960 --> 00:46:50.280
And they want to get out.

00:46:50.280 --> 00:46:54.673
There's no reason for them
to be behind barbwire,

00:46:55.690 --> 00:47:00.490
or to have to spend 90 minutes a day,

00:47:00.490 --> 00:47:03.300
or two hours a day in a yard.

00:47:03.300 --> 00:47:06.570
And we're in the yard
talking to them, right?

00:47:06.570 --> 00:47:08.900
And so, my mind's sort of spinning,

00:47:08.900 --> 00:47:10.780
like this is not even...

00:47:10.780 --> 00:47:13.100
So, the question of political prisoners

00:47:13.100 --> 00:47:15.300
sort of cease to be as prominent

00:47:15.300 --> 00:47:17.530
because you begin to realize

00:47:17.530 --> 00:47:19.793
that there's a politics of imprisonment.

00:47:21.350 --> 00:47:23.850
When you find, when you
hear people's stories,

00:47:23.850 --> 00:47:26.140
sometimes they might make
a mistake, or whatever,

00:47:26.140 --> 00:47:27.050
and they end up in jail.

00:47:27.050 --> 00:47:30.970
A lot of times it's just petty stuff,

00:47:30.970 --> 00:47:33.520
and yet, there are people I know

00:47:33.520 --> 00:47:35.660
who are in my class at the
University of Michigan,

00:47:35.660 --> 00:47:38.520
who do petty stuff that's way worse.

00:47:38.520 --> 00:47:41.290
And they're not in prison, you know?

00:47:41.290 --> 00:47:42.653
They go home in their BMWs,

00:47:44.240 --> 00:47:47.440
and they're paying
out-of-state tuition, whatever.

00:47:47.440 --> 00:47:48.477
So, then it's that.

00:47:48.477 --> 00:47:50.930
And then, all this is swirling,

00:47:50.930 --> 00:47:55.730
and then in 1994, Angela Davis

00:47:55.730 --> 00:47:58.250
is participating in this conference

00:47:58.250 --> 00:48:00.090
called Race Matters.

00:48:00.090 --> 00:48:02.030
And I'm also one of the speakers.

00:48:02.030 --> 00:48:05.080
I'm really young, not so young anymore,

00:48:05.080 --> 00:48:07.800
but so I'm there, Michael R. Dyson,

00:48:07.800 --> 00:48:11.990
a bunch of people, Angela
Davis gives this speech

00:48:11.990 --> 00:48:13.720
and the room is packed.

00:48:13.720 --> 00:48:14.553
This is at Princeton.

00:48:14.553 --> 00:48:18.747
This is in celebration of
Cornell Westbook, Race Matters,

00:48:22.130 --> 00:48:23.670
Race Matters Conference.

00:48:23.670 --> 00:48:27.280
And in this speech, she says,
she basically gives a speech

00:48:27.280 --> 00:48:29.738
that becomes prisons, or prisons obsolete.

00:48:29.738 --> 00:48:30.571
- [Garrett] Mm-hmm.

00:48:30.571 --> 00:48:33.207
- And she says in this packed room,

00:48:33.207 --> 00:48:34.650
and they're there because
she's a celebrity.

00:48:34.650 --> 00:48:35.840
They're there, not 'cause they read any

00:48:35.840 --> 00:48:37.357
of her stuff.

00:48:37.357 --> 00:48:38.190
It's like packed.

00:48:38.190 --> 00:48:40.690
People just on the rafters at Princeton.

00:48:40.690 --> 00:48:44.560
And she says, "It is
time to abolish prisons."

00:48:44.560 --> 00:48:45.620
And you could...

00:48:45.620 --> 00:48:47.854
You could hear a pin drop.

00:48:47.854 --> 00:48:48.687
(male chuckles)

00:48:48.687 --> 00:48:50.960
Those people are like, "What?"

00:48:50.960 --> 00:48:52.440
And I'm there watching the whole thing

00:48:52.440 --> 00:48:53.780
and people lie to you.

00:48:53.780 --> 00:48:55.260
Say people say like I was

00:48:55.260 --> 00:48:56.888
at the March of Washington, they're lying.

00:48:56.888 --> 00:48:57.721
(group laughs)

00:48:57.721 --> 00:48:58.554
You know, it's the same thing.

00:48:58.554 --> 00:48:59.840
They'll say, "Oh no, I was with her."

00:48:59.840 --> 00:49:01.837
No, they weren't.
(group chuckles)

00:49:01.837 --> 00:49:03.510
Nobody was with her.
(group laughs)

00:49:03.510 --> 00:49:04.797
Nobody was with her on that.

00:49:04.797 --> 00:49:05.848
(Derecka claps)

00:49:05.848 --> 00:49:06.890
And these are young people.

00:49:06.890 --> 00:49:09.550
And the first thing they did hand shot up.

00:49:09.550 --> 00:49:11.370
What about the rapists?

00:49:11.370 --> 00:49:14.420
These are black people,
young black students

00:49:14.420 --> 00:49:17.795
at Princeton, the creme de la creme.

00:49:17.795 --> 00:49:19.306
I'm sorry, Yales, the crem de la crem.

00:49:19.306 --> 00:49:20.467
(audience laughs)

00:49:20.467 --> 00:49:22.317
That's secondary.
(audience laughs)

00:49:22.317 --> 00:49:23.400
So, what I'm saying is that

00:49:23.400 --> 00:49:27.157
that was mind blowing because I was there.

00:49:27.157 --> 00:49:28.510
When I say, when I was there,

00:49:28.510 --> 00:49:31.080
I was there politically.

00:49:31.080 --> 00:49:35.737
I'd arrived to completely
agree with her on this.

00:49:35.737 --> 00:49:37.160
And that was 1994.

00:49:37.160 --> 00:49:40.993
So, in some ways I have to give credit,

00:49:41.870 --> 00:49:46.500
not to my own thinking independently.

00:49:46.500 --> 00:49:47.990
It's not even...

00:49:47.990 --> 00:49:50.880
And I wouldn't even say
that it's entirely activism.

00:49:50.880 --> 00:49:52.400
So, that's a part of it.

00:49:52.400 --> 00:49:54.360
Part of it is that I was fortunate

00:49:54.360 --> 00:49:57.080
to be surrounded by
thinkers and intellectuals

00:49:57.080 --> 00:49:58.870
who thought through these things.

00:49:58.870 --> 00:50:02.910
And showed a mirror to what the prisons

00:50:02.910 --> 00:50:04.600
really do to people.

00:50:04.600 --> 00:50:08.140
Between Ruthie, Angela,
these kids at Boysville,

00:50:08.140 --> 00:50:09.810
the folks at Jackson State.

00:50:09.810 --> 00:50:12.551
And then, then of course,
you start to read.

00:50:12.551 --> 00:50:14.910
And finally, the last
thing is just dealing

00:50:14.910 --> 00:50:17.530
with police violence.

00:50:17.530 --> 00:50:21.240
So, Jill Nelson published

00:50:21.240 --> 00:50:24.210
this book called, "Police Brutality,"

00:50:24.210 --> 00:50:26.250
came out in 1999.

00:50:26.250 --> 00:50:27.880
And that book is as relevant today

00:50:27.880 --> 00:50:29.780
as it is, as it was then.

00:50:29.780 --> 00:50:31.690
This is right.

00:50:31.690 --> 00:50:33.640
In fact, it was on the eve of...

00:50:34.630 --> 00:50:36.270
Was it on eve of, or no,

00:50:36.270 --> 00:50:41.030
it was after Amadou
Diallo, around that time.

00:50:41.030 --> 00:50:41.970
And so, the point is,

00:50:41.970 --> 00:50:44.010
is that that book, in that book,

00:50:44.010 --> 00:50:46.100
I kind of made the case at the very end

00:50:46.100 --> 00:50:47.600
of my essay, which is kind of a history

00:50:47.600 --> 00:50:49.690
of police violence, was that we need

00:50:49.690 --> 00:50:51.640
to abolish the police.

00:50:51.640 --> 00:50:53.380
That's 1999, I'm not the first one

00:50:53.380 --> 00:50:54.880
to say that, obviously.

00:50:54.880 --> 00:50:59.320
But my point is that that was a huge move

00:51:00.780 --> 00:51:02.420
and it wasn't, again, it wasn't

00:51:02.420 --> 00:51:06.110
a move from reform to
a revolutionary stance,

00:51:06.110 --> 00:51:06.943
'cause I always...

00:51:06.943 --> 00:51:10.360
I mean, I was a communist.

00:51:10.360 --> 00:51:13.890
I believed in socialism and still do,

00:51:13.890 --> 00:51:14.770
but the point is,

00:51:14.770 --> 00:51:19.330
is that it's amazing how
the carceral structures

00:51:19.330 --> 00:51:21.733
are often taken for granted.

00:51:22.570 --> 00:51:23.930
We talk about the role

00:51:23.930 --> 00:51:28.120
in reproduction of
capital, but not always...

00:51:28.120 --> 00:51:30.190
but there was a belief that somehow

00:51:30.190 --> 00:51:32.790
they could be transformed or reformed,

00:51:32.790 --> 00:51:34.383
because there's some people who need that.

00:51:34.383 --> 00:51:36.860
That we need police, that we need prisons.

00:51:36.860 --> 00:51:40.010
So, the leap is huge.

00:51:40.010 --> 00:51:42.430
And that's why the last thing
I'll just say about this,

00:51:42.430 --> 00:51:43.420
and that's why we have to...

00:51:43.420 --> 00:51:47.210
We have to remember and Derecka makes

00:51:47.210 --> 00:51:48.360
this point in her book,

00:51:49.880 --> 00:51:50.940
that as much as we talk about

00:51:50.940 --> 00:51:53.943
the 13th Amendment and the 14th Amendment,

00:51:54.800 --> 00:51:56.610
but the 13th amendment, the clause

00:51:56.610 --> 00:52:00.410
that says essentially, you could still

00:52:00.410 --> 00:52:03.380
have slavery as long as
someone commits a crime.

00:52:03.380 --> 00:52:08.380
Nobody was questioning
that in 1865, or 66,

00:52:08.570 --> 00:52:11.490
because in fact, Du Bois,

00:52:11.490 --> 00:52:13.730
in his grand black reconstruction

00:52:13.730 --> 00:52:15.600
was very proud of the fact

00:52:15.600 --> 00:52:19.640
that reconstruction moved
from the prison camp

00:52:19.640 --> 00:52:21.350
to the penitentiary.

00:52:21.350 --> 00:52:24.920
That the penitentiary
was the modern iteration,

00:52:24.920 --> 00:52:28.990
the safer, more humane sort of solution

00:52:28.990 --> 00:52:31.360
to deal with prisoners.

00:52:31.360 --> 00:52:33.743
So, we've made a huge leap from that,

00:52:34.661 --> 00:52:35.720
but it's not we're not going back

00:52:35.720 --> 00:52:38.540
to an older abolition,
this is a new abolition.

00:52:38.540 --> 00:52:39.820
And that was a product

00:52:39.820 --> 00:52:41.470
of these kinds of struggles.

00:52:41.470 --> 00:52:45.120
So, that's sort of my
journey, but I just...

00:52:45.120 --> 00:52:48.380
I've stopped there and just simply say,

00:52:48.380 --> 00:52:52.440
we have to always pay attention

00:52:52.440 --> 00:52:54.010
to the institutions that

00:52:54.010 --> 00:52:57.007
have become so normative.
- [Derecka] Mm-hmm.

00:52:57.007 --> 00:52:58.243
- And that's not just the prison,

00:52:58.243 --> 00:53:00.360
it's the family.
- [Derecka] Mm-hmm.

00:53:00.360 --> 00:53:02.110
- There's all kinds of things
that we think about that.

00:53:02.110 --> 00:53:04.469
We think it was normative that
those things are untouchable,

00:53:04.469 --> 00:53:05.790
we just have to fix them.

00:53:05.790 --> 00:53:07.270
So, we have to think
really hard about that.

00:53:07.270 --> 00:53:09.010
So, to be an abolitionist

00:53:09.010 --> 00:53:13.594
is to change everything
as Ruthie would say.

00:53:13.594 --> 00:53:14.427
- [Derecka] Yes.

00:53:14.427 --> 00:53:17.410
- And it's to live life in rehearsal,

00:53:17.410 --> 00:53:19.110
which is also Ruthie's term.

00:53:19.110 --> 00:53:20.454
So, I'll stop there.

00:53:20.454 --> 00:53:23.150
(group applauds)

00:53:23.150 --> 00:53:26.370
- Thank you all for sharing
and for those comments.

00:53:26.370 --> 00:53:27.710
It's a reminder, I think,

00:53:27.710 --> 00:53:30.100
as Garrett said that abolition itself

00:53:30.100 --> 00:53:32.400
isn't linear, that it's
constantly evolving

00:53:32.400 --> 00:53:34.590
and that it grows out of ideas,

00:53:34.590 --> 00:53:36.123
and discussion, and struggle.

00:53:37.290 --> 00:53:38.940
So, three of us up here

00:53:38.940 --> 00:53:41.510
are historians and I would be remiss

00:53:41.510 --> 00:53:44.440
if I didn't invite us
to talk about history.

00:53:44.440 --> 00:53:47.480
And so, Robin and Garrett,
I'd like you to share some

00:53:47.480 --> 00:53:50.500
of your expertise on the history of,

00:53:50.500 --> 00:53:52.320
and struggles for racial justice.

00:53:52.320 --> 00:53:54.810
And Robin, we just read
portions of "Hammer

00:53:54.810 --> 00:53:58.060
and Hoe" in my African
American history survey.

00:53:58.060 --> 00:54:00.180
So, the Alabama Sharecroppers' Union

00:54:00.180 --> 00:54:03.080
has been on my mind a lot lately.

00:54:03.080 --> 00:54:04.960
And I'm wondering if you could

00:54:06.183 --> 00:54:09.040
just help us think through some lessons

00:54:09.040 --> 00:54:11.130
that we can learn about how to organize

00:54:11.130 --> 00:54:14.460
and sustain radical social movement

00:54:14.460 --> 00:54:16.550
from the history of communist organizing

00:54:16.550 --> 00:54:18.660
in the '30s and '40s.

00:54:18.660 --> 00:54:20.940
As well as drawing from other aspects

00:54:20.940 --> 00:54:24.300
of your work, maybe
cultural forms like jazz,

00:54:24.300 --> 00:54:25.133
and hip hop.

00:54:25.133 --> 00:54:27.460
And Garrett, and those who know,

00:54:27.460 --> 00:54:28.460
don't say you introduce

00:54:28.460 --> 00:54:30.970
a concept called the
dialectics of discipline

00:54:30.970 --> 00:54:34.996
as a way to consider the
kind of simultaneous rise

00:54:34.996 --> 00:54:37.440
of collective black resistance,

00:54:37.440 --> 00:54:39.363
and escalating state repression.

00:54:40.380 --> 00:54:42.230
Like increased reliance on solidarity.

00:54:42.230 --> 00:54:45.140
Solitary confinement, and
maximum security prisons

00:54:45.140 --> 00:54:48.070
as a reaction to protest and mobilization

00:54:48.070 --> 00:54:49.470
among incarcerated people.

00:54:49.470 --> 00:54:53.590
And you, in many ways,
all of your work helps

00:54:53.590 --> 00:54:56.610
us see how the carceral
state and the movement

00:54:56.610 --> 00:54:58.860
to abolish it, really rose together.

00:54:58.860 --> 00:55:01.820
So, I guess, it's a similar question

00:55:01.820 --> 00:55:04.060
that I'm asking of Robin,

00:55:04.060 --> 00:55:07.180
and that is what can
abolitionists today learn

00:55:07.180 --> 00:55:10.120
from the struggle of
incarcerated Muslim activists

00:55:10.120 --> 00:55:11.842
in the '50s and '60s?

00:55:11.842 --> 00:55:14.880
And where do we see the dialectics

00:55:14.880 --> 00:55:16.703
of discipline in prisons today?

00:55:18.210 --> 00:55:19.587
- Well, Garrett can go first.

00:55:19.587 --> 00:55:21.340
(audience chatters and chuckles)

00:55:21.340 --> 00:55:22.736
- Well chronologically.
(chuckles)

00:55:22.736 --> 00:55:23.899
(panelists chatter indistinctly)

00:55:23.899 --> 00:55:24.732
- Oh.

00:55:24.732 --> 00:55:25.565
(Elizabeth laughs)

00:55:25.565 --> 00:55:26.398
No, you need to be in

00:55:26.398 --> 00:55:27.231
the history question too, no.

00:55:27.231 --> 00:55:28.064
- Yeah, yeah.

00:55:28.064 --> 00:55:28.897
- I have-
- No.

00:55:28.897 --> 00:55:29.730
- Well, I have a third-

00:55:29.730 --> 00:55:31.377
- I figured there was
another question. (laughs)

00:55:31.377 --> 00:55:32.650
- I'll pose that now.

00:55:32.650 --> 00:55:33.483
I'll pose it now.

00:55:33.483 --> 00:55:35.240
So, the question that I had for Derecka,

00:55:35.240 --> 00:55:37.340
I think, everybody can comment

00:55:37.340 --> 00:55:38.173
on these questions,

00:55:38.173 --> 00:55:41.630
but since you're the lawyer among us,

00:55:41.630 --> 00:55:43.083
she's the only one on stage with a JD.

00:55:43.083 --> 00:55:44.650
- Barely, Jesus.

00:55:44.650 --> 00:55:46.530
- I would really love for you

00:55:46.530 --> 00:55:49.360
to reflect on the relationship

00:55:49.360 --> 00:55:50.880
between abolition and the law.

00:55:50.880 --> 00:55:52.760
And as you know well,

00:55:52.760 --> 00:55:54.160
the law has really served

00:55:54.160 --> 00:55:55.080
to kind of facilitate

00:55:55.080 --> 00:55:57.540
and legitimate state violence

00:55:57.540 --> 00:55:58.720
against marginalized groups.

00:55:58.720 --> 00:56:01.470
But it also has functioned at times

00:56:01.470 --> 00:56:03.500
as an instrument of
protection and justice.

00:56:03.500 --> 00:56:05.270
So, slavery is abolished, we get Jim Crow.

00:56:05.270 --> 00:56:08.096
Jim Crow's abolished, we
get mass criminalization.

00:56:08.096 --> 00:56:08.929
- Mm-hmm.

00:56:08.929 --> 00:56:10.970
- And I'm wondering what
limits and possibilities

00:56:10.970 --> 00:56:14.610
the law might offer for abolition.

00:56:14.610 --> 00:56:16.133
- Mm-hmm, right.

00:56:17.360 --> 00:56:18.240
- The law and the state,

00:56:18.240 --> 00:56:19.150
'cause that also came up.

00:56:19.150 --> 00:56:19.983
- Yeah.

00:56:19.983 --> 00:56:22.618
- I mean, do you want me
to go first, 'cause...

00:56:22.618 --> 00:56:23.451
- [Elizabeth] Anybody-

00:56:23.451 --> 00:56:24.442
(crosstalk drowns out speaker)

00:56:24.442 --> 00:56:25.275
- Cause I'll be actually, I'm not gonna...

00:56:25.275 --> 00:56:26.413
I'll be very brief.

00:56:26.413 --> 00:56:27.670
- [Elizabeth] Okay.

00:56:27.670 --> 00:56:29.450
- I can't answer the second question,

00:56:29.450 --> 00:56:31.530
which is kind of cultural forms,

00:56:31.530 --> 00:56:33.481
but I will talk about specifically

00:56:33.481 --> 00:56:36.730
the Communist Party and
the Sharecroppers' Union,

00:56:36.730 --> 00:56:39.400
because I think if there's a lesson there,

00:56:39.400 --> 00:56:40.233
I mean, we have to remember

00:56:40.233 --> 00:56:41.883
that these movements were crushed.

00:56:42.810 --> 00:56:44.010
I mean, crushed to the point

00:56:44.010 --> 00:56:46.633
where there was almost no memory.

00:56:48.390 --> 00:56:51.562
And so, we have to be prepared

00:56:51.562 --> 00:56:54.480
in terms of the longevity movements

00:56:54.480 --> 00:56:57.650
to recognize that that
there's a dialectic,

00:56:57.650 --> 00:56:59.550
there's ebbs and flows.

00:56:59.550 --> 00:57:02.520
And we wouldn't have had, I mean,

00:57:02.520 --> 00:57:03.840
for the Sharecroppers' Union

00:57:03.840 --> 00:57:07.470
and for the labor organizing in the 1930s,

00:57:07.470 --> 00:57:08.820
I mean the truth of the matter

00:57:08.820 --> 00:57:12.210
is that it took a lot of
courage, mobilization,

00:57:12.210 --> 00:57:13.621
a sense of crisis,

00:57:13.621 --> 00:57:18.621
but it also took space
that the state created.

00:57:19.170 --> 00:57:20.740
And we don't always talk about this,

00:57:20.740 --> 00:57:23.390
but as problematic as the new deal was

00:57:23.390 --> 00:57:26.320
and as racist as it was, certain things

00:57:26.320 --> 00:57:28.380
were products of worker struggles.

00:57:28.380 --> 00:57:29.870
And this goes back to the question

00:57:29.870 --> 00:57:32.390
that you posed about mutual aid,

00:57:32.390 --> 00:57:34.480
versus the struggle of state.
- [Derecka] Mm-hmm.

00:57:34.480 --> 00:57:36.381
- That they're not necessarily
mutually exclusive.

00:57:36.381 --> 00:57:37.370
- [Derecka] Mm-hmm.

00:57:37.370 --> 00:57:39.490
- That the struggle to create

00:57:39.490 --> 00:57:44.320
a National Labor Relations Board,

00:57:44.320 --> 00:57:46.020
which of course, doesn't
affect Sharecroppers',

00:57:46.020 --> 00:57:47.570
'cause they're not even covered, right?

00:57:47.570 --> 00:57:50.360
But that did create space

00:57:50.360 --> 00:57:53.780
for unionizing and for pushing back

00:57:53.780 --> 00:57:55.803
against companies that create,

00:57:56.820 --> 00:57:59.640
basically company unions to undermine

00:57:59.640 --> 00:58:00.930
the organizing efforts.

00:58:00.930 --> 00:58:02.360
In terms of the Sharecroppers',

00:58:02.360 --> 00:58:03.458
they may not have had

00:58:03.458 --> 00:58:06.950
the National Labor
Relations Board behind them,

00:58:06.950 --> 00:58:09.040
but they had a couple things behind them.

00:58:09.040 --> 00:58:11.170
The belief that with all

00:58:11.170 --> 00:58:12.823
this dynamic organizing going on,

00:58:12.823 --> 00:58:16.672
that they also need to
be out there organizing.

00:58:16.672 --> 00:58:19.430
Even if it meant engaging

00:58:19.430 --> 00:58:21.060
in shootouts with police.

00:58:21.060 --> 00:58:23.560
And then secondly, the most amazing thing,

00:58:23.560 --> 00:58:25.410
this has to do with internationalism.

00:58:26.879 --> 00:58:28.600
The most successful movements

00:58:28.600 --> 00:58:30.360
in the 20th century are those

00:58:30.360 --> 00:58:31.850
that actually either

00:58:31.850 --> 00:58:35.100
have connections beyond borders.

00:58:35.100 --> 00:58:35.950
- [Derecka] Mm-hmm.

00:58:35.950 --> 00:58:37.930
- Or believe they have connections.

00:58:37.930 --> 00:58:38.950
You don't have to have, actually

00:58:38.950 --> 00:58:40.180
have real connections,

00:58:40.180 --> 00:58:42.168
but what's beautiful about

00:58:42.168 --> 00:58:43.001
the Communist Party in Alabama

00:58:43.001 --> 00:58:46.650
is that they're like, we are an extension

00:58:46.650 --> 00:58:49.090
of the Soviet power.

00:58:49.090 --> 00:58:51.820
And we know, and they said this,

00:58:51.820 --> 00:58:53.317
I mean, some people I interviewed said,

00:58:53.317 --> 00:58:56.440
"We knew that when we
went on strike in 1935,

00:58:56.440 --> 00:58:58.210
if anything were to happen to us,

00:58:58.210 --> 00:59:00.010
that Stalin would send troops.

00:59:00.010 --> 00:59:02.180
He would send ships across the Atlantic

00:59:02.180 --> 00:59:04.330
into mobile and troops would come

00:59:04.330 --> 00:59:07.460
into Alabama and protect us.

00:59:07.460 --> 00:59:08.920
That's solidarity.

00:59:08.920 --> 00:59:11.990
Now, that didn't happen,
but the belief in it,

00:59:11.990 --> 00:59:13.370
the belief that somehow, you're part

00:59:13.370 --> 00:59:15.470
of something larger and
that the working class

00:59:15.470 --> 00:59:17.862
is not a national class.

00:59:17.862 --> 00:59:19.757
It's an international class.

00:59:19.757 --> 00:59:20.730
And this was...

00:59:20.730 --> 00:59:23.937
This has always been the Achilles heel

00:59:23.937 --> 00:59:27.480
of American working class struggles,

00:59:27.480 --> 00:59:28.671
both as nationalism

00:59:28.671 --> 00:59:30.253
and nationalism translates into what?

00:59:30.253 --> 00:59:31.720
- [Derecka] Mm-hmm.
- White nationalism.

00:59:31.720 --> 00:59:32.553
- [Derecka] Mm-hmm.

00:59:32.553 --> 00:59:35.360
- It is black workers, workers of color

00:59:35.360 --> 00:59:37.250
have been always at the
forefront of saying,

00:59:37.250 --> 00:59:39.353
we see ourselves as a class.

00:59:39.353 --> 00:59:41.040
It's the white workers that make

00:59:41.040 --> 00:59:43.830
the mistake of seeing
themselves as a race.

00:59:43.830 --> 00:59:45.920
And when they see themselves as a race,

00:59:45.920 --> 00:59:47.980
then they end up joining the wrong side.

00:59:47.980 --> 00:59:51.250
And this was the message
that the boys taught us.

00:59:51.250 --> 00:59:54.010
And this is the message of the 1930s,

00:59:54.010 --> 00:59:56.374
because what happened was once you had

00:59:56.374 --> 00:59:58.910
the wind of the federal
government behind your back,

00:59:58.910 --> 01:00:00.490
or belief in it,

01:00:00.490 --> 01:00:03.360
all the white folks,
who started to realize

01:00:03.360 --> 01:00:04.453
that wait a second,

01:00:06.100 --> 01:00:08.871
we don't have any other
option but to organize.

01:00:08.871 --> 01:00:11.120
And they actually were able to win over

01:00:11.120 --> 01:00:12.890
a segment of white working class.

01:00:12.890 --> 01:00:16.560
And I think, for us in the 21st century,

01:00:16.560 --> 01:00:18.550
this is important, and I'm not making

01:00:18.550 --> 01:00:21.360
a case against "identity politics",

01:00:21.360 --> 01:00:23.620
'cause my belief in
identity politics goes back

01:00:23.620 --> 01:00:25.659
to Barbara Smith.

01:00:25.659 --> 01:00:28.810
And combine, they have a
different understanding of it.

01:00:28.810 --> 01:00:31.540
But I do think it's important not

01:00:31.540 --> 01:00:33.920
to get caught up in the silos,

01:00:33.920 --> 01:00:37.725
and to build power, means to basically,

01:00:37.725 --> 01:00:41.350
you don't have to give up identity,

01:00:41.350 --> 01:00:43.730
but you do to build power across

01:00:43.730 --> 01:00:45.123
the lines of these silos.

01:00:45.123 --> 01:00:48.850
And sometimes, building power
means to mobilize people,

01:00:48.850 --> 01:00:51.360
who at first, are against you.

01:00:51.360 --> 01:00:52.820
It means mobilizing people

01:00:52.820 --> 01:00:55.092
who may share your same interests-

01:00:55.092 --> 01:00:55.925
- Mm-hmm.

01:00:55.925 --> 01:00:58.280
- Politically, but they don't know better.

01:00:58.280 --> 01:01:00.637
What's the point of political education?

01:01:00.637 --> 01:01:01.553
- Right.
- Right?

01:01:01.553 --> 01:01:04.240
It's a political to basically get people

01:01:04.240 --> 01:01:07.000
to agree with you and get them in the room

01:01:07.000 --> 01:01:10.164
and say, "Yeah, Walter Rodney, you know?"

01:01:10.164 --> 01:01:10.997
(group chuckles)

01:01:10.997 --> 01:01:12.530
Or is it to try to build people

01:01:12.530 --> 01:01:14.123
who don't really know?

01:01:16.460 --> 01:01:20.390
Because the thing is,
it's not as if the state

01:01:20.390 --> 01:01:23.470
is picking and choosing who they're trying

01:01:23.470 --> 01:01:26.130
to bring into their realm, right?

01:01:26.130 --> 01:01:27.830
So, we have to think in those terms,

01:01:27.830 --> 01:01:30.080
and those are the lessons
to me of the 1930s.

01:01:33.170 --> 01:01:34.920
- [Garrett] Where are we
going, chronologically?

01:01:34.920 --> 01:01:36.150
- [Elizabeth] Yeah-

01:01:36.150 --> 01:01:36.983
(crosstalk drowns out speaker)

01:01:36.983 --> 01:01:38.430
- [Garrett] Okay, so
I'm all over the place.

01:01:38.430 --> 01:01:39.980
I think, I'm gonna interpret

01:01:39.980 --> 01:01:42.700
the question as sort of a broader,

01:01:42.700 --> 01:01:44.540
like what can we learn from the struggles

01:01:44.540 --> 01:01:45.743
of imprisoned people?

01:01:46.820 --> 01:01:48.100
'Cause I think that I'm thinking

01:01:48.100 --> 01:01:50.858
of the new project too, and just what

01:01:50.858 --> 01:01:51.691
you all are saying.

01:01:51.691 --> 01:01:55.270
And I mean, for me, not even historically,

01:01:55.270 --> 01:01:58.440
just in this moment,
like what thinking about

01:01:58.440 --> 01:02:00.360
the struggles of imprisoned
people has allowed

01:02:00.360 --> 01:02:03.490
me to do is see everything
else differently.

01:02:03.490 --> 01:02:06.420
So, I see the university differently

01:02:06.420 --> 01:02:08.980
because I understand its relationship

01:02:08.980 --> 01:02:11.580
to policing and prisons.

01:02:11.580 --> 01:02:12.750
And that's been useful for me.

01:02:12.750 --> 01:02:14.530
I mean, when we think about

01:02:14.530 --> 01:02:15.934
these sort of campaigns

01:02:15.934 --> 01:02:19.080
to have cops off campus, right?

01:02:19.080 --> 01:02:22.420
Yale can't exist without
police in prisons.

01:02:22.420 --> 01:02:25.070
It's not just about eradicating police

01:02:25.070 --> 01:02:27.094
from the campus, right?

01:02:27.094 --> 01:02:31.770
The only way that Yale can operate

01:02:31.770 --> 01:02:35.790
through dispossession,
through incarceration,

01:02:35.790 --> 01:02:39.280
through imprisonment, through land seizure

01:02:39.280 --> 01:02:42.740
is through police and prisons.

01:02:42.740 --> 01:02:46.750
So, when you read George Jackson talking

01:02:46.750 --> 01:02:48.720
about fascism-
- [Derecka] Yeah, yeah, yes.

01:02:48.720 --> 01:02:50.770
- Reform, right?

01:02:50.770 --> 01:02:53.670
It's like, people don't think

01:02:53.670 --> 01:02:56.190
we're living in a fascist police state,

01:02:56.190 --> 01:02:58.340
but it's because you don't feel

01:02:58.340 --> 01:03:01.166
the fascist police state the way

01:03:01.166 --> 01:03:03.102
that Lawrence feels it.

01:03:03.102 --> 01:03:03.935
- [Robin] Mm-hmm.

01:03:03.935 --> 01:03:04.768
- The way that Stevie feels it.

01:03:04.768 --> 01:03:05.601
- [Robin] Right.

01:03:05.601 --> 01:03:06.490
- They would think it's ridiculous

01:03:06.490 --> 01:03:07.860
to say we are not living in

01:03:07.860 --> 01:03:09.690
a fascist police state.

01:03:09.690 --> 01:03:12.150
Stevie can't receive his writings.

01:03:12.150 --> 01:03:13.880
He writes things that are published

01:03:13.880 --> 01:03:15.475
in the so-called free world

01:03:15.475 --> 01:03:18.600
that he can then not receive back.

01:03:18.600 --> 01:03:19.440
That's fascism.

01:03:19.440 --> 01:03:20.330
- [Robin] Fascism.

01:03:20.330 --> 01:03:21.340
- But it's not fascism

01:03:21.340 --> 01:03:22.173
if we don't feel it, right?

01:03:22.173 --> 01:03:23.853
That's what we think.

01:03:27.160 --> 01:03:28.940
I talk about collective dreaming.

01:03:28.940 --> 01:03:30.240
This project that I'm writing

01:03:30.240 --> 01:03:33.320
that Robin and Derecka
were so gracious to read.

01:03:33.320 --> 01:03:35.670
I try to share my
writings on Martin Sostre,

01:03:35.670 --> 01:03:38.200
who's this revolutionary anarchist,

01:03:38.200 --> 01:03:39.960
political prisoner with people inside.

01:03:39.960 --> 01:03:41.033
They can't read it.

01:03:42.030 --> 01:03:45.800
So, there's all this kind of like, talk

01:03:45.800 --> 01:03:48.040
about censorship and anti CRT,

01:03:48.040 --> 01:03:49.290
and book bannings.

01:03:49.290 --> 01:03:51.890
It's happening every day, I promise you.

01:03:51.890 --> 01:03:54.850
If you are a politicized prisoner,

01:03:54.850 --> 01:03:59.020
everything you do is mediated through

01:03:59.020 --> 01:04:01.780
the state, through fascism.

01:04:01.780 --> 01:04:04.790
So, I think what's valuable about not

01:04:04.790 --> 01:04:06.740
just studying these struggles,

01:04:06.740 --> 01:04:09.200
but being in solidarity
with and in community

01:04:09.200 --> 01:04:12.320
with people is you're able to understand

01:04:12.320 --> 01:04:14.090
the way that all these unseen forces

01:04:14.090 --> 01:04:15.950
of criminalization and incarceration

01:04:15.950 --> 01:04:19.130
are shaping this.

01:04:19.130 --> 01:04:21.530
In a way that you wouldn't
understand otherwise.

01:04:24.490 --> 01:04:27.111
I don't know if I answered
your question at all.

01:04:27.111 --> 01:04:28.063
- [Robin] Yeah.

01:04:28.063 --> 01:04:28.896
(audience chuckles)
(crosstalk drowns out speaker)

01:04:28.896 --> 01:04:29.729
It's a great answer.

01:04:29.729 --> 01:04:30.562
- Okay.

01:04:30.562 --> 01:04:31.395
- No, it's important.

01:04:31.395 --> 01:04:34.300
It's important, I'm just thinking

01:04:34.300 --> 01:04:38.059
about so much about where to even...

01:04:38.059 --> 01:04:42.743
Where to even start, so
abolition and the law.

01:04:42.743 --> 01:04:44.870
(sighs) The law is kind of trash.

01:04:44.870 --> 01:04:46.177
I don't know.

01:04:46.177 --> 01:04:47.010
(audience laughs)

01:04:47.010 --> 01:04:47.850
I don't know, I'm trying to figure out

01:04:47.850 --> 01:04:49.940
where's the best way to even start.

01:04:49.940 --> 01:04:51.403
Is anyone in here in law school,

01:04:51.403 --> 01:04:53.000
or want to go to law school,

01:04:53.000 --> 01:04:55.324
or walk passed a law school?

01:04:55.324 --> 01:04:56.157
(group laughs)

01:04:56.157 --> 01:04:56.990
- [Elizabeth] There are some,

01:04:56.990 --> 01:04:57.823
but they're not the reason they're here.

01:04:57.823 --> 01:04:58.656
- Some, okay, yeah.

01:04:58.656 --> 01:04:59.489
(Elizabeth laughs)

01:04:59.489 --> 01:05:00.989
- Oh, no, it's so interesting.

01:05:02.141 --> 01:05:03.770
(sighs) Where do you start this?

01:05:03.770 --> 01:05:06.260
So, are you asking if they're compatible,

01:05:06.260 --> 01:05:07.460
are they relation to each other?

01:05:07.460 --> 01:05:08.780
- [Elizabeth] I'm just
wondering how you see,

01:05:08.780 --> 01:05:09.613
I mean, 'cause you're a law...

01:05:09.613 --> 01:05:11.680
You're an abolition, I mean, and you have

01:05:11.680 --> 01:05:12.926
a law degree, you are a lawyer.

01:05:12.926 --> 01:05:14.643
- I do have a law degree.
- [Elizabeth] So, what are-

01:05:14.643 --> 01:05:15.476
(crosstalk drowns out speaker)

01:05:15.476 --> 01:05:17.900
How can the law be a
tool, or is it just trash?

01:05:17.900 --> 01:05:19.090
I mean, maybe it's just trash and it's-

01:05:19.090 --> 01:05:20.730
- Well, yeah, well some trash is useful.

01:05:20.730 --> 01:05:21.563
That's why we go-

01:05:21.563 --> 01:05:22.600
(group laughs)
Let me get this out,

01:05:22.600 --> 01:05:24.712
or I wish I hadn't thrifted that shirt.

01:05:24.712 --> 01:05:26.580
(group laughs)
So, it's complicated.

01:05:26.580 --> 01:05:28.360
Yes, so what can I say?

01:05:28.360 --> 01:05:32.213
I think that, yes, I've...

01:05:32.213 --> 01:05:34.440
Sometimes we talk about politicization

01:05:34.440 --> 01:05:36.700
as like an event that
happens is in our lives,

01:05:36.700 --> 01:05:38.820
and then we're like,
"Whoa," but no, people

01:05:38.820 --> 01:05:40.780
are constantly experiencing
different levels

01:05:40.780 --> 01:05:41.950
of being politicized

01:05:41.950 --> 01:05:44.620
when we're introducing different politics.

01:05:44.620 --> 01:05:46.990
And so, I was politicized in abolition,

01:05:46.990 --> 01:05:50.450
mostly in law school after
I already entered, right?

01:05:50.450 --> 01:05:53.180
So, I talk about in the book

01:05:53.180 --> 01:05:55.100
that Michael Brown was
killed about five minutes

01:05:55.100 --> 01:05:56.640
from my house, about three weeks before

01:05:56.640 --> 01:05:57.473
I started law school.

01:05:57.473 --> 01:06:01.130
And so, I drove from
St. Louis to Cambridge,

01:06:01.130 --> 01:06:02.680
trying to figure out how to wrap

01:06:02.680 --> 01:06:06.340
my head around the movement
that was happening outside.

01:06:06.340 --> 01:06:08.570
And then, you go to class,
and then, you have professors

01:06:08.570 --> 01:06:10.238
who don't talk about it.

01:06:10.238 --> 01:06:11.071
- [Robin] Mm.

01:06:11.071 --> 01:06:11.904
- And then, you spend a year

01:06:11.904 --> 01:06:13.680
in Dean of Students offices crying

01:06:13.680 --> 01:06:14.720
so they can send an email

01:06:14.720 --> 01:06:17.510
to acknowledge how violent
and painful this is.

01:06:17.510 --> 01:06:19.210
To try to study for exams while

01:06:19.210 --> 01:06:21.720
their are dead bodies going
viral on social media.

01:06:21.720 --> 01:06:25.215
So, that that was like, what is going on?

01:06:25.215 --> 01:06:30.215
Why am I learning about this
at this particular moment?

01:06:30.530 --> 01:06:31.780
I think, what was interesting

01:06:31.780 --> 01:06:33.630
when I told you all when I came back,

01:06:33.630 --> 01:06:37.120
and I asked the students
I was organizing with.

01:06:37.120 --> 01:06:38.375
There were a lot of us,

01:06:38.375 --> 01:06:39.660
there were maybe like 100 students,

01:06:39.660 --> 01:06:42.860
who were actively engaged on a daily basis

01:06:42.860 --> 01:06:45.010
of trying to figure out how to connect

01:06:45.010 --> 01:06:47.660
what we were learning to
what was happening outside.

01:06:47.660 --> 01:06:49.160
And I said, "Hey, y'all, I think that

01:06:49.160 --> 01:06:50.830
we should probably do political education,

01:06:50.830 --> 01:06:52.780
'cause I don't think a diversity office

01:06:52.780 --> 01:06:54.430
is gonna help change this.

01:06:54.430 --> 01:06:57.630
I don't think that hiring
a critical race theorist

01:06:57.630 --> 01:06:59.660
is gonna actually fundamentally shake up

01:06:59.660 --> 01:07:02.110
the things that we're fighting for.

01:07:02.110 --> 01:07:04.230
I think that we should read and study,

01:07:04.230 --> 01:07:05.500
and think critically about

01:07:05.500 --> 01:07:06.910
what we're trying to accomplish,

01:07:06.910 --> 01:07:09.063
especially since we're
gonna be future lawyers.

01:07:09.063 --> 01:07:12.170
And after lots of tense debates,

01:07:12.170 --> 01:07:14.071
our movement split.

01:07:14.071 --> 01:07:18.320
At least half of us left,
or half of that group left.

01:07:18.320 --> 01:07:20.010
And they were like, I
just wanna sit around

01:07:20.010 --> 01:07:23.170
and read, and affirm each
other about how right you are.

01:07:23.170 --> 01:07:25.340
Y'all not trying to actually
win, or change Harvard.

01:07:25.340 --> 01:07:26.570
So, we're gonna leave,

01:07:26.570 --> 01:07:29.590
and the rest of us entered
us into a student occupation

01:07:29.590 --> 01:07:31.520
for the next year and a half.

01:07:31.520 --> 01:07:34.100
So, much so I graduated five years ago

01:07:34.100 --> 01:07:36.630
and people still who go to HOS call

01:07:36.630 --> 01:07:38.387
the space we occupy, Belinda Hall.

01:07:38.387 --> 01:07:41.180
That is what we mean
when we claim that space,

01:07:41.180 --> 01:07:43.330
which I think, is somewhat noteworthy.

01:07:43.330 --> 01:07:44.660
But what was so beautiful

01:07:44.660 --> 01:07:46.560
about Belinda Hall and us going through

01:07:46.560 --> 01:07:48.290
our political education process,

01:07:48.290 --> 01:07:49.900
it became the only space on campus

01:07:49.900 --> 01:07:52.050
where anyone could teach, or take a class.

01:07:52.050 --> 01:07:52.883
It didn't matter if you were

01:07:52.883 --> 01:07:54.010
a dining services worker.

01:07:54.010 --> 01:07:55.140
It didn't matter if you were a janitor,

01:07:55.140 --> 01:07:55.973
it didn't matter if you were

01:07:55.973 --> 01:07:57.870
a clinical faculty, you could teach,

01:07:57.870 --> 01:07:59.370
or take a class, right?

01:07:59.370 --> 01:08:01.490
It's where organizers from Boston

01:08:01.490 --> 01:08:02.980
will come in and do, and train us

01:08:02.980 --> 01:08:04.500
on organizing techniques, right?

01:08:04.500 --> 01:08:06.890
It was a place where anyone could come

01:08:06.890 --> 01:08:10.020
and eat is where we, said
we don't want police here.

01:08:10.020 --> 01:08:12.500
I mean, police were
showing up to our visuals,

01:08:12.500 --> 01:08:15.100
like to Boston, the black
Lawsuit Association,

01:08:15.100 --> 01:08:17.710
when we would have candles
and people were crying.

01:08:17.710 --> 01:08:18.710
It would be police.

01:08:18.710 --> 01:08:20.630
And when you said that places at Yale

01:08:20.630 --> 01:08:22.550
don't exist without the police,

01:08:22.550 --> 01:08:24.220
it's sort of a concrete thing.

01:08:24.220 --> 01:08:25.727
And so, to me, it's not always like,

01:08:25.727 --> 01:08:27.880
"What about the murderers,
and what about the rapists?"

01:08:27.880 --> 01:08:29.781
That's not why police primarily exist.

01:08:29.781 --> 01:08:30.614
- [Robin] Right.

01:08:30.614 --> 01:08:33.708
- They exist to manage
inequality and protect borders,

01:08:33.708 --> 01:08:37.160
like internal borders,
external borders, right?

01:08:37.160 --> 01:08:40.340
So, there are people who struggle

01:08:40.340 --> 01:08:42.100
with that question so much, Elizabeth,

01:08:42.100 --> 01:08:46.840
about how do I grapple with
these new sets of understanding

01:08:46.840 --> 01:08:49.760
that I have about the
world and my role in it.

01:08:49.760 --> 01:08:52.547
And then, reconcile that
with becoming a lawyer.

01:08:52.547 --> 01:08:53.430
And there are people who gave up

01:08:53.430 --> 01:08:54.710
their firm jobs.

01:08:54.710 --> 01:08:57.350
People gave up $200,000 a year

01:08:57.350 --> 01:09:00.400
to pay back their student debt
and became public defenders,

01:09:00.400 --> 01:09:01.954
or intend to public service work.

01:09:01.954 --> 01:09:04.660
There are some people who
said, I can't be a lawyer.

01:09:04.660 --> 01:09:05.810
I can't reconcile this.

01:09:05.810 --> 01:09:08.730
And they're a label
organizer still right now.

01:09:08.730 --> 01:09:11.540
And so, people grapple with
it in a lot of different ways.

01:09:11.540 --> 01:09:13.450
I told, I mean, Robin
knows this, every year

01:09:13.450 --> 01:09:14.786
I have a crisis.

01:09:14.786 --> 01:09:16.180
I'm like, should I be in a PhD program?

01:09:16.180 --> 01:09:17.770
So, people were struggling with like,

01:09:17.770 --> 01:09:19.567
can I actually be a lawyer?

01:09:19.567 --> 01:09:21.289
And I think, the reason why I chose

01:09:21.289 --> 01:09:23.870
to study for that horrible thing called

01:09:23.870 --> 01:09:26.870
the bar exam, and then become a lawyer,

01:09:26.870 --> 01:09:29.140
is because I knew that there were people

01:09:29.140 --> 01:09:31.160
in St. Louis who were figuring out

01:09:31.160 --> 01:09:35.030
how to put pressure on the
Ferguson Police Department.

01:09:35.030 --> 01:09:37.250
Through this with the Department

01:09:37.250 --> 01:09:39.320
of Justice called a consent decree,

01:09:39.320 --> 01:09:41.620
and being a lawyer was one way

01:09:41.620 --> 01:09:42.660
that I could help them.

01:09:42.660 --> 01:09:44.000
And I already took out the debt.

01:09:44.000 --> 01:09:45.420
I was like, you know what?

01:09:45.420 --> 01:09:48.210
I'm gonna have to pay
back over $100,000 anyway,

01:09:48.210 --> 01:09:49.800
might as well try it out a little bit.

01:09:49.800 --> 01:09:51.660
And so, that's sort of what happened,

01:09:51.660 --> 01:09:52.493
and I was...

01:09:52.493 --> 01:09:54.250
I felt very lucky and very fortunate

01:09:54.250 --> 01:09:55.187
to be able to do that.

01:09:55.187 --> 01:09:57.350
And so, can you a tool?

01:09:57.350 --> 01:09:59.520
Absolutely, I mean, if you're a lawyer,

01:09:59.520 --> 01:10:01.000
you can get people outta jail,

01:10:01.000 --> 01:10:02.470
or you can help people litigate.

01:10:02.470 --> 01:10:06.650
You can do what I do,
which is those things,

01:10:06.650 --> 01:10:09.090
but also, help think about policy

01:10:09.090 --> 01:10:10.750
in a particular way to put it.

01:10:10.750 --> 01:10:13.131
Your help gets bills drafted, right?

01:10:13.131 --> 01:10:14.130
So, I think that's important.

01:10:14.130 --> 01:10:15.067
I think that people in

01:10:15.067 --> 01:10:16.870
our community do those things as well,

01:10:16.870 --> 01:10:18.070
who are not lawyers.

01:10:18.070 --> 01:10:20.020
There's people who do
participatory defense.

01:10:20.020 --> 01:10:21.620
There are people who represent themselves

01:10:21.620 --> 01:10:23.220
and get people outta jail.

01:10:23.220 --> 01:10:24.920
I mean, the law's intentionally written

01:10:24.920 --> 01:10:26.960
in a particular language, so that people

01:10:26.960 --> 01:10:28.310
who take out hundreds of thousands

01:10:28.310 --> 01:10:31.270
of student loan debt can articulate that

01:10:31.270 --> 01:10:32.103
and then, make good on it.

01:10:32.103 --> 01:10:34.627
So, it's really good job security, right?

01:10:34.627 --> 01:10:35.960
But I'm always inspired

01:10:35.960 --> 01:10:38.810
when I watch community based organizations

01:10:38.810 --> 01:10:39.840
do court watching.

01:10:39.840 --> 01:10:42.080
They're not, those people are not lawyers

01:10:42.080 --> 01:10:42.913
and what do they do?

01:10:42.913 --> 01:10:44.210
Every Monday in Kansas City we have 10

01:10:44.210 --> 01:10:46.390
and two, go and watch eviction court.

01:10:46.390 --> 01:10:47.710
And because of their presence,

01:10:47.710 --> 01:10:49.130
because of their organizing,

01:10:49.130 --> 01:10:52.970
they put pressure on the judge
to delay, or stop evictions.

01:10:52.970 --> 01:10:55.077
You don't need a law
degree to do that, right?

01:10:55.077 --> 01:10:56.550
So, I didn't know that before

01:10:56.550 --> 01:10:59.100
I went to law school, but
the more that I lawyer,

01:10:59.100 --> 01:11:01.060
the more that I organize,
the more that I write,

01:11:01.060 --> 01:11:03.450
the more that I am in the social movement,

01:11:03.450 --> 01:11:07.350
I learn how people decide to use the law.

01:11:07.350 --> 01:11:09.760
But more importantly, how
to use their presence,

01:11:09.760 --> 01:11:13.390
and their organizing as
tactics to manipulate the law

01:11:13.390 --> 01:11:15.160
as a non-reformist reform.

01:11:15.160 --> 01:11:16.510
Something that doesn't give

01:11:16.510 --> 01:11:18.700
it more power and more legitimacy,

01:11:18.700 --> 01:11:21.140
but actually, it's trying
to decrease the violence,

01:11:21.140 --> 01:11:23.700
or at least, mitigate what
it's doing in the world.

01:11:23.700 --> 01:11:25.207
So, I think, that's important,

01:11:25.207 --> 01:11:27.730
and it's the answer I'm excited to give,

01:11:27.730 --> 01:11:29.470
which is different than a question

01:11:29.470 --> 01:11:30.470
I'm usually asked,

01:11:30.470 --> 01:11:33.040
which is can I be an
abolitionist and a lawyer?

01:11:33.040 --> 01:11:34.290
- [Robin] Mm-hmm.

01:11:34.290 --> 01:11:35.220
- All right, that's the question

01:11:35.220 --> 01:11:37.850
I feel like I'm asked the most at schools.

01:11:37.850 --> 01:11:40.000
Say, "Can I do these things?"

01:11:40.000 --> 01:11:42.400
I was like, "Well, I don't know."

01:11:42.400 --> 01:11:44.250
I think, again, more important

01:11:44.250 --> 01:11:47.520
than you identifying as
an abolitionist lawyer

01:11:47.520 --> 01:11:48.770
is that you try to be

01:11:48.770 --> 01:11:52.000
a part of a political
organization, and lawyer,

01:11:52.000 --> 01:11:53.780
and try to figure out if you can use

01:11:53.780 --> 01:11:56.520
your skills towards that broader movement.

01:11:56.520 --> 01:11:58.150
And what's so fascinating,

01:11:58.150 --> 01:11:59.990
the work that I do in that capacity

01:11:59.990 --> 01:12:01.347
is not my lawyering work,

01:12:01.347 --> 01:12:03.160
and I don't get paid for it, right?

01:12:03.160 --> 01:12:05.670
I don't get paid to be on phone calls

01:12:05.670 --> 01:12:07.220
for 15 hours a week,

01:12:07.220 --> 01:12:08.830
thinking about student debt cancellation

01:12:08.830 --> 01:12:09.930
with Dream Defenders.

01:12:09.930 --> 01:12:11.760
That's not, I didn't go
to Harvard Law School

01:12:11.760 --> 01:12:13.390
in order to do that, but that's something

01:12:13.390 --> 01:12:14.330
that's something important to me,

01:12:14.330 --> 01:12:16.530
'cause I know that once we figure out

01:12:16.530 --> 01:12:18.932
how to make sure that we can cancel

01:12:18.932 --> 01:12:21.000
and abolish all kinds of debts.

01:12:21.000 --> 01:12:23.920
Fight for universal healthcare,
fight for free education,

01:12:23.920 --> 01:12:27.170
it increases agency for people
to live the kind of lives

01:12:27.170 --> 01:12:28.850
that they wanna live and we deserve it.

01:12:28.850 --> 01:12:30.130
And so, yeah, I...

01:12:30.130 --> 01:12:35.030
I mean, yeah, I think
everyone on this panel

01:12:35.030 --> 01:12:38.210
will know that the way that so many

01:12:38.210 --> 01:12:39.790
of our hopes and dreams,

01:12:39.790 --> 01:12:43.450
especially given the
professionalization push

01:12:43.450 --> 01:12:46.019
in the '80s and '90s.

01:12:46.019 --> 01:12:48.510
If you wanna go do good in the world

01:12:48.510 --> 01:12:50.500
to channel that through a career,

01:12:50.500 --> 01:12:52.860
through a specific career,

01:12:52.860 --> 01:12:55.477
it's like, "Yeah, I go do this thing."

01:12:55.477 --> 01:12:57.566
And the right's not doing that.

01:12:57.566 --> 01:12:58.399
- [Robin] Right.

01:12:58.399 --> 01:12:59.443
- The right's not doing that.

01:13:00.730 --> 01:13:03.950
- I know you said earlier,
what did you just say?

01:13:03.950 --> 01:13:05.730
It reminded me of this,

01:13:05.730 --> 01:13:06.920
that essentially the doors

01:13:06.920 --> 01:13:08.837
of white supremacy are always open.

01:13:08.837 --> 01:13:09.670
- Right.

01:13:09.670 --> 01:13:10.503
(audience chuckles)

01:13:10.503 --> 01:13:11.336
- That any moment, it's kind of like...

01:13:11.336 --> 01:13:12.169
I'm serious-
- We were talking about it.

01:13:12.169 --> 01:13:13.860
- At any moment, if you wanna go...

01:13:13.860 --> 01:13:15.360
Oh, yeah we were talking about it, though

01:13:15.360 --> 01:13:16.623
with the white workers.

01:13:17.680 --> 01:13:19.120
At any moment, if you want to join

01:13:19.120 --> 01:13:21.760
a white supremacist organization, you can.

01:13:21.760 --> 01:13:24.120
You can go do this.

01:13:24.120 --> 01:13:26.050
It's not, I mean, you obviously become

01:13:26.050 --> 01:13:28.300
a cop or into the military,

01:13:28.300 --> 01:13:29.730
into any one of these other positions,

01:13:29.730 --> 01:13:31.830
but they are building,

01:13:31.830 --> 01:13:33.413
that's grass was organizing, right?

01:13:33.413 --> 01:13:35.490
They're doing, this is
stuff they're doing also

01:13:35.490 --> 01:13:36.830
in their spare time.

01:13:36.830 --> 01:13:38.290
And so, sometimes I'm nervous

01:13:38.290 --> 01:13:39.530
that people who care about freedom

01:13:39.530 --> 01:13:41.000
and justice think that they can only do

01:13:41.000 --> 01:13:42.870
that through a specific career.

01:13:42.870 --> 01:13:44.360
And it's like, "No, you need to be

01:13:44.360 --> 01:13:45.720
a part of a political organization,

01:13:45.720 --> 01:13:47.270
or figure out how to support one."

01:13:47.270 --> 01:13:49.107
That's how we build and sustain movements

01:13:49.107 --> 01:13:50.550
for the long term.

01:13:50.550 --> 01:13:53.480
And I'm nervous about the decline of that,

01:13:53.480 --> 01:13:56.210
especially as I look at what's happening

01:13:56.210 --> 01:13:58.140
with progressives on the left,

01:13:58.140 --> 01:13:59.930
it's like everyone is doing this through

01:13:59.930 --> 01:14:01.100
their policy job.

01:14:01.100 --> 01:14:03.470
And no one's doing this
through political struggle.

01:14:03.470 --> 01:14:05.580
And even though we hear 20 million people

01:14:05.580 --> 01:14:07.690
were pouring into the
streets for a protest,

01:14:07.690 --> 01:14:09.670
that's cool, but 20 million people

01:14:09.670 --> 01:14:12.013
did not get absorbed into organizations.

01:14:12.013 --> 01:14:12.846
- Mm.

01:14:12.846 --> 01:14:14.030
- 20 million people are not outside

01:14:14.030 --> 01:14:15.537
the White House when Biden says,

01:14:15.537 --> 01:14:16.610
"I'm gonna gonna keep choosing

01:14:16.610 --> 01:14:17.580
to refund the police."

01:14:17.580 --> 01:14:19.570
20 million people are not outside

01:14:19.570 --> 01:14:21.270
of Rikers saying, let these people out,

01:14:21.270 --> 01:14:23.070
'cause they're dying rapidly.

01:14:23.070 --> 01:14:25.480
And so, that energy that
we get excited about

01:14:25.480 --> 01:14:28.500
when we get those glimpses,
that gets called a movement,

01:14:28.500 --> 01:14:31.230
but movements require so
much more sustainability

01:14:31.230 --> 01:14:33.350
and organizing then those moments.

01:14:33.350 --> 01:14:35.240
And so, we need people and I now,

01:14:35.240 --> 01:14:36.584
I'm like, this is like alter call.

01:14:36.584 --> 01:14:39.250
We need people to join organizations,

01:14:39.250 --> 01:14:40.083
or at least to figure out

01:14:40.083 --> 01:14:41.870
how to support them so we can fight

01:14:41.870 --> 01:14:43.810
and build something much more sustainable.

01:14:43.810 --> 01:14:45.657
- [Elizabeth] Right, thank you.

01:14:45.657 --> 01:14:47.681
Thank you all for that, and
thanks for that, Derecka.

01:14:47.681 --> 01:14:48.514
(group applauds)

01:14:48.514 --> 01:14:51.100
I mean, and I think,
too I just wanna end it

01:14:51.100 --> 01:14:54.250
on a historical lesson,
because that's what we see.

01:14:54.250 --> 01:14:56.230
That's what sustained...
That's what sustained

01:14:56.230 --> 01:14:57.063
the freedom movement historically.

01:14:57.063 --> 01:14:58.290
- Yeah, yeah, yes.

01:14:58.290 --> 01:15:00.950
It's been organizing,
it's been sustaining it.

01:15:00.950 --> 01:15:04.170
It's been not just mass mobilizations.

01:15:04.170 --> 01:15:05.620
Okay, so I wanna...

01:15:05.620 --> 01:15:07.160
I have a million more questions,

01:15:07.160 --> 01:15:08.220
but we're running out of time.

01:15:08.220 --> 01:15:10.800
So, I really wanna open
it up to everyone else.

01:15:10.800 --> 01:15:13.090
So, please start coming down

01:15:13.090 --> 01:15:15.240
to the microphones to ask questions,

01:15:15.240 --> 01:15:19.500
and while you do that, I'm
gonna pose one from online,

01:15:19.500 --> 01:15:21.860
from Polly, and then, kind of tack one

01:15:21.860 --> 01:15:23.370
of my own questions onto it.

01:15:23.370 --> 01:15:26.790
So, Polly asked, "How
can we who have lived

01:15:26.790 --> 01:15:28.470
our entire lives within empire,

01:15:28.470 --> 01:15:31.160
create live inter political relationships

01:15:31.160 --> 01:15:33.250
that don't replicate oppressive patterns?"

01:15:33.250 --> 01:15:35.520
And I know that's a really,

01:15:35.520 --> 01:15:37.530
really important, very broad question.

01:15:37.530 --> 01:15:39.380
I would also kind of based on where

01:15:39.380 --> 01:15:41.770
the conversation is headed, invite you

01:15:41.770 --> 01:15:44.900
to also reflect on what universities

01:15:44.900 --> 01:15:48.030
and what university
communities can and should do.

01:15:48.030 --> 01:15:50.590
I mean, we're here at Yale University,

01:15:50.590 --> 01:15:53.680
which just had an incident endowment

01:15:53.680 --> 01:15:56.480
over the past year go up by $10 billion.

01:15:56.480 --> 01:15:58.080
And yet, it gets...

01:15:58.080 --> 01:15:59.550
The people of New Haven

01:15:59.550 --> 01:16:01.690
basically (chuckles) subsidized Yale

01:16:01.690 --> 01:16:04.073
at 160 million a year.

01:16:04.960 --> 01:16:09.470
And 34% of the kids in New Haven are at,

01:16:09.470 --> 01:16:12.290
or below the poverty level.
- Oh, my God.

01:16:12.290 --> 01:16:17.290
- So, how can we think
about abolishing structures

01:16:17.370 --> 01:16:21.523
that we are very much tied to and within?

01:16:22.530 --> 01:16:23.543
From me and Polly.

01:16:28.720 --> 01:16:30.360
- Well, I think, the last thing you said

01:16:30.360 --> 01:16:33.604
was the answer was in the...

01:16:33.604 --> 01:16:36.492
In the question, I mean, we have

01:16:36.492 --> 01:16:39.122
just to talk about Yale, this is...

01:16:39.122 --> 01:16:42.763
This is an old issue, and of
course, Yale's not unique,

01:16:44.370 --> 01:16:46.900
but you have these land grant institutions

01:16:46.900 --> 01:16:48.563
that don't pay taxes.

01:16:50.183 --> 01:16:53.300
Where there's a intense
union struggle going on

01:16:53.300 --> 01:16:55.173
all the time for living wage.

01:16:57.070 --> 01:16:59.560
And I'm at a... And I teach at a school

01:16:59.560 --> 01:17:03.113
where the basketball
coach, makes way more money

01:17:05.830 --> 01:17:06.863
than anyone else.

01:17:09.049 --> 01:17:10.980
And so, there's some basic things,

01:17:10.980 --> 01:17:13.570
like if you think about reparations

01:17:17.800 --> 01:17:21.140
and decolonization, take these two themes.

01:17:21.140 --> 01:17:25.040
Reparations would mean that
those institutions like Yale,

01:17:25.040 --> 01:17:29.820
that have been exploiting
people on stolen land

01:17:29.820 --> 01:17:34.590
for three centuries owe something.

01:17:34.590 --> 01:17:38.370
And so, if you include everything

01:17:38.370 --> 01:17:40.630
that they've been able to extract

01:17:40.630 --> 01:17:42.110
and to figure out a way
to sort of take that

01:17:42.110 --> 01:17:43.740
and give it back, that's one thing,

01:17:43.740 --> 01:17:46.480
and this is maybe a legal regime for that,

01:17:46.480 --> 01:17:48.373
but decolonization is another thing.

01:17:49.840 --> 01:17:50.830
In terms of the question

01:17:50.830 --> 01:17:55.420
about how do you not
replicate oppressive systems,

01:17:55.420 --> 01:17:58.420
something as basic as fighting

01:17:58.420 --> 01:18:03.070
for indigenous sovereignty
can change a lot

01:18:03.070 --> 01:18:06.050
of things in terms of
not just the environment,

01:18:06.050 --> 01:18:09.533
but in terms of working
conditions, housing.

01:18:11.992 --> 01:18:14.160
'Cause I'd much rather
be under sovereign rule

01:18:14.160 --> 01:18:15.920
of indigenous peoples than I would under

01:18:15.920 --> 01:18:17.003
the United States.

01:18:18.960 --> 01:18:20.850
So, these are all opportunities,

01:18:20.850 --> 01:18:22.610
and that basically means each one

01:18:22.610 --> 01:18:24.550
of those things means two things.

01:18:24.550 --> 01:18:27.040
It means the massive redistribution

01:18:27.040 --> 01:18:30.290
of wealth and resources that
have been extracted and stolen.

01:18:30.290 --> 01:18:34.630
And two, it means some complete
transformation of governance

01:18:34.630 --> 01:18:37.330
and that may seem like pie in the sky,

01:18:37.330 --> 01:18:39.623
but there's precedent for that.

01:18:40.464 --> 01:18:41.881
I dunno if you...

01:18:44.600 --> 01:18:46.190
- Why y'all looking at me, I don't know.

01:18:46.190 --> 01:18:48.090
Well, (chuckles) just kidding.

01:18:48.090 --> 01:18:53.090
Well, I... What can people
be doing at the university?

01:18:53.330 --> 01:18:56.540
Well, I think, I wanna say organize,

01:18:56.540 --> 01:18:57.730
we need people to organize.

01:18:57.730 --> 01:18:58.930
I think, it's very exciting

01:18:58.930 --> 01:19:03.200
when I see students and faculty
and staff organize together.

01:19:03.200 --> 01:19:05.300
Another major lesson I
learned in South Africa

01:19:05.300 --> 01:19:08.380
was just the students
and the staff struggle

01:19:08.380 --> 01:19:10.570
were United for intentional reasons,

01:19:10.570 --> 01:19:13.413
because staff were in precarious position.

01:19:14.310 --> 01:19:16.120
They were being shifted

01:19:16.120 --> 01:19:17.930
to becoming contractual laborers,

01:19:17.930 --> 01:19:19.710
which means that they lost healthcare

01:19:19.710 --> 01:19:21.600
and tuition benefits, and all the things

01:19:21.600 --> 01:19:24.010
that you really fight for, fought for

01:19:24.010 --> 01:19:25.377
to be a part of the university.

01:19:25.377 --> 01:19:27.227
And so, those struggles are so important

01:19:27.227 --> 01:19:30.540
and it makes me think
about the early formations

01:19:30.540 --> 01:19:34.010
of the first black study
programs in the '60s.

01:19:34.010 --> 01:19:37.070
Those black student
organizations that were forming,

01:19:37.070 --> 01:19:38.930
they weren't forming for students,

01:19:38.930 --> 01:19:41.950
anyone could kind of come
and be a part of them.

01:19:41.950 --> 01:19:45.450
When they took over campus San
Francisco, State University,

01:19:45.450 --> 01:19:48.700
those students, they went,
the black students and staff,

01:19:48.700 --> 01:19:50.770
they went into the admission offices

01:19:50.770 --> 01:19:52.460
and admitted black students,

01:19:52.460 --> 01:19:55.030
because the university wouldn't
admit black students, right?

01:19:55.030 --> 01:19:57.000
It's like, this is the context.

01:19:57.000 --> 01:19:58.080
Sometimes I'm nervous

01:19:58.080 --> 01:20:00.300
that because we don't
think about the length

01:20:00.300 --> 01:20:02.720
that people went through
for political struggle,

01:20:02.720 --> 01:20:06.800
the possibilities seem
like much more shallow.

01:20:06.800 --> 01:20:07.633
I think it would be...

01:20:07.633 --> 01:20:09.190
What would it look like for students here

01:20:09.190 --> 01:20:10.740
to call for a moratorium?

01:20:10.740 --> 01:20:13.020
Just a moratorium, just to say, "Hey,

01:20:13.020 --> 01:20:15.150
let's stop for a second, let's pause

01:20:15.150 --> 01:20:18.210
on expansion of Yale in New Haven."

01:20:18.210 --> 01:20:20.120
It's a very... That's
a low hanging from...

01:20:20.120 --> 01:20:22.331
That's not even all the way to me calling.

01:20:22.331 --> 01:20:23.630
It seems like, hey, no more.

01:20:23.630 --> 01:20:24.980
Yesterday, when I was leaving

01:20:24.980 --> 01:20:29.050
the event last night, I
got into an Uber ride,

01:20:29.050 --> 01:20:31.470
and the driver was playing T. Pain,

01:20:31.470 --> 01:20:32.640
which was really nice.

01:20:32.640 --> 01:20:34.570
It was, I felt warm and welcomed.

01:20:34.570 --> 01:20:36.570
I was like, okay, this
is like, this is good.

01:20:36.570 --> 01:20:38.470
And I just started asking her questions

01:20:38.470 --> 01:20:40.550
about what it's like being in New Haven.

01:20:40.550 --> 01:20:42.656
And she said, "Well, all
my friends are leaving,

01:20:42.656 --> 01:20:44.287
'cause we can't afford
to live here no more.

01:20:44.287 --> 01:20:46.260
And I was like, "Where
are they moving to?"

01:20:46.260 --> 01:20:48.090
She didn't say New York.

01:20:48.090 --> 01:20:49.980
She didn't say DC.

01:20:49.980 --> 01:20:54.490
She said North Carolina,
South Carolina and Georgia.

01:20:54.490 --> 01:20:57.210
And I said, "Is it because of the cost

01:20:57.210 --> 01:21:00.710
of living that people keep
going further and further?"

01:21:00.710 --> 01:21:02.287
She said, "Yeah," she said,

01:21:02.287 --> 01:21:04.030
"You can't live around here."

01:21:04.030 --> 01:21:05.090
And I was like, "Why not?"

01:21:05.090 --> 01:21:07.520
And she said, I think she had just...

01:21:07.520 --> 01:21:08.353
I think, she was probably

01:21:08.353 --> 01:21:10.140
a police officer, or a security guard,

01:21:10.140 --> 01:21:12.229
she had on her uniform.

01:21:12.229 --> 01:21:15.080
She said, "I don't know, I hear activists

01:21:15.080 --> 01:21:18.695
be using words like
gentrification, but I..."

01:21:18.695 --> 01:21:20.260
I didn't know what gentrification was,

01:21:20.260 --> 01:21:21.820
but in my entire life I knew

01:21:21.820 --> 01:21:24.100
that it was happening, all right?

01:21:24.100 --> 01:21:25.960
And I was like, "Oh, you
gotta join an organization."

01:21:25.960 --> 01:21:28.050
Then I just started doing
my little organizing spiel

01:21:28.050 --> 01:21:28.950
for her too.
(chuckles)

01:21:28.950 --> 01:21:31.240
She was like, oh, maybe
that's not like my thing,

01:21:31.240 --> 01:21:32.460
but people are gonna fight for it,

01:21:32.460 --> 01:21:34.410
but Yale is just gonna win.

01:21:34.410 --> 01:21:35.770
Yale is just gonna win.

01:21:35.770 --> 01:21:37.900
And I was like, "Well you still gotta try,

01:21:37.900 --> 01:21:38.733
we still gotta try."

01:21:38.733 --> 01:21:40.820
So, when I see students trying

01:21:40.820 --> 01:21:43.110
to do that, student, staff, faculty.

01:21:43.110 --> 01:21:43.980
There are students right now

01:21:43.980 --> 01:21:45.420
who are a student at Harvard over

01:21:45.420 --> 01:21:47.860
their endowment because there's invested

01:21:47.860 --> 01:21:50.210
in prisons and things that contribute

01:21:50.210 --> 01:21:51.043
to fossil fuels.

01:21:51.043 --> 01:21:51.950
I think, that's exciting.

01:21:51.950 --> 01:21:53.980
I think, the Cops Off Campus Initiative,

01:21:53.980 --> 01:21:55.080
I think, that was pretty exciting.

01:21:55.080 --> 01:21:57.030
I think, people who are doing inside

01:21:57.030 --> 01:21:58.090
and outside programs with people

01:21:58.090 --> 01:22:00.750
who are incarcerated, I
think, that's important.

01:22:00.750 --> 01:22:02.400
I think, yeah, I think
that that's all enough.

01:22:02.400 --> 01:22:04.380
I think, I mean, I don't
think that's enough.

01:22:04.380 --> 01:22:06.200
I think, those a good starts,

01:22:06.200 --> 01:22:08.000
but I really do think it's gonna depend

01:22:08.000 --> 01:22:10.430
on what inspired you to get activated

01:22:10.430 --> 01:22:12.070
and doing that political education

01:22:12.070 --> 01:22:13.970
with other people to figure out, okay,

01:22:13.970 --> 01:22:15.673
what's the long term goal?

01:22:16.670 --> 01:22:17.503
I'm gonna say this really quickly,

01:22:17.503 --> 01:22:20.510
but Garrett, I keep
thinking about what you say,

01:22:20.510 --> 01:22:22.170
Yale wouldn't be Yale.

01:22:22.170 --> 01:22:24.010
And before we split that student group,

01:22:24.010 --> 01:22:25.320
I was talking about

01:22:25.320 --> 01:22:27.170
when we were reconfiguring our demands,

01:22:27.170 --> 01:22:29.210
and we said, "We need to rethink

01:22:29.210 --> 01:22:31.007
our admissions policy."

01:22:31.960 --> 01:22:34.510
We need to rethink
tuition, all this stuff.

01:22:34.510 --> 01:22:36.187
And one of the students said,

01:22:36.187 --> 01:22:37.990
"Well, would this even be Harvard

01:22:37.990 --> 01:22:39.759
if we had open admissions?"

01:22:39.759 --> 01:22:40.592
- Right.

01:22:40.592 --> 01:22:43.190
- And had such a fear of losing status,

01:22:43.190 --> 01:22:44.480
and we were fighting

01:22:44.480 --> 01:22:47.460
for again, very low hanging fruit.

01:22:47.460 --> 01:22:50.220
We wanted the shield of Harvard Law

01:22:50.220 --> 01:22:52.590
to change because it belonged to a family

01:22:52.590 --> 01:22:54.910
that bequeathed money to
start Harvard Law School,

01:22:54.910 --> 01:22:58.013
that got that money, 'cause
he was a slaver, right?

01:22:58.013 --> 01:23:00.660
Fled during the revolutionary war,

01:23:00.660 --> 01:23:02.570
but gave the money to Harvard Law School.

01:23:02.570 --> 01:23:04.070
And when people, when we fought

01:23:04.070 --> 01:23:06.680
to even get a shield change, a picture

01:23:06.680 --> 01:23:07.970
that wasn't even that cute.

01:23:07.970 --> 01:23:09.463
It wasn't even that cute.

01:23:09.463 --> 01:23:10.421
(audience chuckles)

01:23:10.421 --> 01:23:12.280
But there were alum, including black alum,

01:23:12.280 --> 01:23:15.370
who said, "Oh, is my degree gonna mean

01:23:15.370 --> 01:23:16.203
the same thing?"

01:23:16.203 --> 01:23:18.400
Is my diploma gonna mean the same thing

01:23:18.400 --> 01:23:19.969
if you guys get a new shield?

01:23:19.969 --> 01:23:20.802
- Yeah.

01:23:20.802 --> 01:23:21.635
- Whoa, whoa, whoa, come on.

01:23:21.635 --> 01:23:24.460
And then, our professors,
what did they say?

01:23:24.460 --> 01:23:28.790
Well, if we want to change the shield,

01:23:28.790 --> 01:23:30.820
then if you carry that line on through,

01:23:30.820 --> 01:23:32.080
we're just gonna have to change all

01:23:32.080 --> 01:23:33.868
the symbols in the United States.

01:23:33.868 --> 01:23:36.010
(audience chatters and laughs)

01:23:36.010 --> 01:23:37.941
I said, "Is that a critique or a plan?"

01:23:37.941 --> 01:23:38.893
(group laughs)
'Cause that's our plan.

01:23:39.815 --> 01:23:41.897
I said, "Yeah."
- Yeah, why not?

01:23:41.897 --> 01:23:43.786
- Yeah, why not? I said,
"This sounds good."

01:23:43.786 --> 01:23:45.723
I was like, "You trying to come
to the meeting on Thursday?"

01:23:45.723 --> 01:23:46.681
(audience laughs)

01:23:46.681 --> 01:23:49.560
Right, and so, there's
a deep fear of loss.

01:23:49.560 --> 01:23:53.100
And that deep fear of loss is
being conflated with erasure

01:23:53.100 --> 01:23:54.590
and protecting history.

01:23:54.590 --> 01:23:57.100
And we have to say, no,
this is decolonization.

01:23:57.100 --> 01:23:58.310
And we have to figure out how

01:23:58.310 --> 01:24:03.140
to build more systemic
movement to name that.

01:24:03.140 --> 01:24:04.680
We can affirm the loss for some people,

01:24:04.680 --> 01:24:06.170
we can't say just because you afraid

01:24:06.170 --> 01:24:09.140
of losing status, that this
is not worth the fight.

01:24:09.140 --> 01:24:10.543
- Mm-hmm, that's right.

01:24:11.930 --> 01:24:14.360
- Let's take a question from the audience,

01:24:14.360 --> 01:24:17.330
from Nasar, who's a student
in my Afghan student class.

01:24:17.330 --> 01:24:19.086
Thank you for being the first.

01:24:19.086 --> 01:24:21.260
(group laughs)

01:24:21.260 --> 01:24:22.680
- Peace, family.

01:24:22.680 --> 01:24:26.170
So, yeah, Nasar in
Professor Hinton's class.

01:24:26.170 --> 01:24:27.670
And I really enjoyed the panel.

01:24:27.670 --> 01:24:29.660
Thank you everyone for coming
(mumbles indistinctly),

01:24:29.660 --> 01:24:34.660
but I'm kind of involved with...

01:24:34.740 --> 01:24:37.530
As a community organizer for
Black Lives Matter Patterson,

01:24:37.530 --> 01:24:39.020
and we're really heavily involved

01:24:39.020 --> 01:24:41.420
with the mutual aid that
you guys talking about.

01:24:42.650 --> 01:24:45.400
We were very thankful,
but like every Friday,

01:24:45.400 --> 01:24:47.470
we go out in the community, right?

01:24:47.470 --> 01:24:50.610
Police ridden, mass incarceration,

01:24:50.610 --> 01:24:52.154
just the violence is there.

01:24:52.154 --> 01:24:55.320
And giving, it's much
more than giving food.

01:24:55.320 --> 01:24:58.240
It's also about telling stories,

01:24:58.240 --> 01:25:01.630
and joy, and laughter, right?

01:25:01.630 --> 01:25:05.707
And so, it really is
like a movement of love

01:25:05.707 --> 01:25:08.300
at the basis, which is at the center

01:25:08.300 --> 01:25:10.020
for my opinion, of the political education

01:25:10.020 --> 01:25:11.440
that we're getting at.

01:25:11.440 --> 01:25:14.070
But maybe in your experiences,

01:25:14.070 --> 01:25:16.980
both with research and
on the streets activism,

01:25:16.980 --> 01:25:18.680
what do you think the role is

01:25:18.680 --> 01:25:21.380
in the importance of love and building

01:25:21.380 --> 01:25:23.303
that in the abolitionist movement?

01:25:24.872 --> 01:25:26.247
- Such a good question (indistinct).

01:25:29.250 --> 01:25:31.018
- I mean, Rodney, yeah, I mean...

01:25:31.018 --> 01:25:32.310
(male chuckles)
It's at the center,

01:25:32.310 --> 01:25:35.500
I think, is what... I mean,
that's like Robin's article

01:25:35.500 --> 01:25:39.820
that I mentioned earlier,
"Love Study Struggle."

01:25:39.820 --> 01:25:41.350
- [Robin] Mm-hmm.

01:25:41.350 --> 01:25:43.477
- And I just think that love

01:25:43.477 --> 01:25:48.010
is related to trust and relationships,

01:25:48.010 --> 01:25:52.643
which are at the core of
struggle and organizing.

01:25:54.982 --> 01:25:58.070
And I think, another component maybe

01:25:58.070 --> 01:26:00.720
that I'll just push out
there that's related

01:26:00.720 --> 01:26:03.430
to love is about commitment.

01:26:03.430 --> 01:26:04.670
And I don't mean commitment

01:26:04.670 --> 01:26:06.810
to a set of principles, but a commitment

01:26:06.810 --> 01:26:11.810
to people to be around and to not dip in

01:26:11.810 --> 01:26:12.670
and dip out.

01:26:12.670 --> 01:26:16.080
That you have to maintain
lifelong relationships

01:26:16.080 --> 01:26:19.233
with people and that love
can sound really good,

01:26:20.210 --> 01:26:21.940
and it can also be really hard.

01:26:21.940 --> 01:26:22.773
- [Derecka] Mm-hmm.

01:26:22.773 --> 01:26:23.640
- [Robin] Right.
- Right, and we think

01:26:23.640 --> 01:26:27.170
about how hard love is in our families,

01:26:27.170 --> 01:26:30.020
or our intimate relationships,

01:26:30.020 --> 01:26:34.130
but political love for long periods

01:26:34.130 --> 01:26:35.720
of time can be really hard.

01:26:35.720 --> 01:26:37.853
I mean, people hurt each other.

01:26:39.010 --> 01:26:41.480
People betray each other.

01:26:41.480 --> 01:26:44.680
So, I think, if that's not at the core

01:26:44.680 --> 01:26:46.590
of the organizing that we do,

01:26:46.590 --> 01:26:48.240
it can fall apart really fast.

01:26:48.240 --> 01:26:50.850
And there's that energy
when you start organizing

01:26:50.850 --> 01:26:52.620
and you feel like there's
love, and all this stuff,

01:26:52.620 --> 01:26:55.990
and it can get really tough fast, I think.

01:26:55.990 --> 01:27:00.750
Once you enter a kind
of period of maturity.

01:27:00.750 --> 01:27:01.583
- Right.

01:27:01.583 --> 01:27:03.320
- And I would also add this,

01:27:03.320 --> 01:27:04.860
there's three kinds of love.

01:27:04.860 --> 01:27:05.950
There's eros, which

01:27:05.950 --> 01:27:08.030
we sometimes conflate with love.

01:27:08.030 --> 01:27:09.680
There's philos, which is friendship,

01:27:09.680 --> 01:27:11.435
and then there's agape.

01:27:11.435 --> 01:27:13.650
So, when I talk about love

01:27:13.650 --> 01:27:15.050
and I think, a lot of us talk about love,

01:27:15.050 --> 01:27:17.090
we're thinking in terms of agape,

01:27:17.090 --> 01:27:22.090
which Dr. King is drawing on people to it.

01:27:22.410 --> 01:27:26.110
Calls the constant, relentless struggle

01:27:26.110 --> 01:27:30.160
to make community, and no distinction

01:27:30.160 --> 01:27:31.943
between friend and enemy.

01:27:32.840 --> 01:27:34.490
That's the hardest thing.

01:27:34.490 --> 01:27:36.870
Now, we may know who, what forces

01:27:36.870 --> 01:27:40.670
were right against and that's different.

01:27:40.670 --> 01:27:43.610
But the idea is that you try to create

01:27:43.610 --> 01:27:47.110
a community that is completely inclusive.

01:27:47.110 --> 01:27:50.080
And even the people that
you don't agree with,

01:27:50.080 --> 01:27:53.090
you pull them in, not for consensus,

01:27:53.090 --> 01:27:54.210
'cause it's not about consensus.

01:27:54.210 --> 01:27:56.630
It's about constant
struggle to make community,

01:27:56.630 --> 01:27:58.640
which means that you debate, you struggle,

01:27:58.640 --> 01:28:01.190
you fight, you critique
until you could come

01:28:01.190 --> 01:28:05.840
to a place of agreement,

01:28:05.840 --> 01:28:08.070
which we know is always temporary.

01:28:08.070 --> 01:28:09.270
So, just like Garrett was saying,

01:28:09.270 --> 01:28:12.170
it's very, very hard
to do, but you can't...

01:28:12.170 --> 01:28:14.620
There's no such thing as
a movement without love.

01:28:16.180 --> 01:28:18.180
I mean, the irony is that even the Klan,

01:28:20.090 --> 01:28:23.520
they operate on a basis of really demonic

01:28:23.520 --> 01:28:24.786
kind of love.

01:28:24.786 --> 01:28:26.510
(Derecka and audience chuckle)

01:28:26.510 --> 01:28:30.090
And it's about, it's not even on the map,

01:28:30.090 --> 01:28:32.603
that kind of love, but it's vicious.

01:28:35.910 --> 01:28:37.833
- Yeah, I agree. I agree.

01:28:38.690 --> 01:28:40.970
It's definitely been what Robin

01:28:40.970 --> 01:28:42.990
just said about the constant struggle

01:28:42.990 --> 01:28:46.060
to build community and a better society

01:28:46.060 --> 01:28:47.930
for the people who may even disagree

01:28:47.930 --> 01:28:49.620
with you, or who may hate you,

01:28:49.620 --> 01:28:51.004
or may not dislike you.

01:28:51.004 --> 01:28:53.940
It's yes, it's hard, so much so.

01:28:53.940 --> 01:28:56.260
I actually, chapter four in the book

01:28:56.260 --> 01:28:58.630
is called love and
abolition for that reason.

01:28:58.630 --> 01:29:00.880
'Cause I care deeply about what

01:29:00.880 --> 01:29:02.100
that struggle takes.

01:29:02.100 --> 01:29:04.790
And I try to show this is where I talk

01:29:04.790 --> 01:29:06.820
about that split in the student movement

01:29:06.820 --> 01:29:07.723
and how hard that was.

01:29:07.723 --> 01:29:09.900
This is where I talk about representing

01:29:09.900 --> 01:29:11.937
a client that I had, who was very,

01:29:11.937 --> 01:29:14.040
very racist toward me,

01:29:14.040 --> 01:29:15.447
and toward other people, right?

01:29:15.447 --> 01:29:17.370
And I had to stand next to this person

01:29:17.370 --> 01:29:19.300
in court and vigorously defend

01:29:19.300 --> 01:29:20.133
that person, right?

01:29:20.133 --> 01:29:22.250
This is where I talk about

01:29:22.250 --> 01:29:26.960
the black reconstruction and how Du Bois

01:29:26.960 --> 01:29:28.910
sort of focuses on abolitionist,

01:29:28.910 --> 01:29:31.020
primarily why abolitionist
is in the critique

01:29:31.020 --> 01:29:32.840
of Du Bois for missing out

01:29:32.840 --> 01:29:34.970
the deep revolutionary love and practices.

01:29:34.970 --> 01:29:38.660
That black insurrection did to flee,

01:29:38.660 --> 01:29:41.010
and build communities in the face

01:29:41.010 --> 01:29:42.253
of white supremacist violence.

01:29:42.253 --> 01:29:44.900
And so, I think, love
has an essential place,

01:29:44.900 --> 01:29:47.120
and I hope my comments about mutual aid,

01:29:47.120 --> 01:29:48.710
I'm hearing what you're saying.

01:29:48.710 --> 01:29:50.970
I'm not anti-mutual aid.

01:29:50.970 --> 01:29:52.720
I am very pro mutual aid.

01:29:52.720 --> 01:29:55.265
I heart mutual aid, very, very big.

01:29:55.265 --> 01:29:56.220
(audience laughs)

01:29:56.220 --> 01:29:58.010
In the book, I affirm mutual aid.

01:29:58.010 --> 01:30:00.170
And I said, "I think, it's
very, very important."

01:30:00.170 --> 01:30:01.800
I think, one of my earlier comments,

01:30:01.800 --> 01:30:04.010
what I was saying is that I'm nervous that

01:30:05.758 --> 01:30:10.400
there's a shift I'm
experiencing among my peers,

01:30:10.400 --> 01:30:12.320
and people who are
actually younger than me

01:30:12.320 --> 01:30:16.350
to mutual aid, out of
desperation and pessimism.

01:30:16.350 --> 01:30:18.330
Which is not the same as shifting

01:30:18.330 --> 01:30:19.163
to it out of love.

01:30:19.163 --> 01:30:21.140
So, I have a lot of
love for BLM Patterson.

01:30:21.140 --> 01:30:23.330
I think, that's yo, Zoey, the whole crew.

01:30:23.330 --> 01:30:24.163
I think y'all are doing...

01:30:24.163 --> 01:30:26.103
Exactly, I know. I know, see?

01:30:26.103 --> 01:30:26.936
(audience chuckles)

01:30:26.936 --> 01:30:28.210
I think, that work is very important.

01:30:28.210 --> 01:30:29.450
The free refrigerators

01:30:29.450 --> 01:30:30.500
where people can come get food.

01:30:30.500 --> 01:30:32.920
I get it, I know we
have to keep doing that.

01:30:32.920 --> 01:30:34.180
I don't think Zoey and everyone there

01:30:34.180 --> 01:30:36.310
is organizing from that place, right?

01:30:36.310 --> 01:30:38.380
But I'm nervous about that becoming

01:30:38.380 --> 01:30:40.083
a widespread thing, that's all.

01:30:41.228 --> 01:30:42.061
- [Nasar] Thank you.

01:30:42.061 --> 01:30:42.894
- Yeah, no, I knew D.G.

01:30:42.894 --> 01:30:44.103
I know he looks surprised. (laughs)

01:30:44.103 --> 01:30:47.020
(Elizabeth laughs)

01:30:48.561 --> 01:30:50.730
- [Female] (mumbles) hello, everyone.

01:30:50.730 --> 01:30:51.820
Hi, I have a question

01:30:51.820 --> 01:30:53.340
