01:31:24.905 --> 01:31:25.827
Okay.
- Okay, (chuckles) sorry.

01:31:25.827 --> 01:31:26.660
(panelists chatter indistinctly)

01:31:26.660 --> 01:31:29.410
(group chuckles)

01:31:31.243 --> 01:31:33.604
Sorry, that was my fault.
- Okay, all right.

01:31:33.604 --> 01:31:35.037
Okay, is this...

01:31:35.037 --> 01:31:35.870
- Yeah, that's much better, yeah.

01:31:35.870 --> 01:31:37.550
- Wow, amazing, okay, thank you.

01:31:37.550 --> 01:31:39.410
Hi, everyone, I have a question

01:31:39.410 --> 01:31:40.290
from a comrade inside.

01:31:40.290 --> 01:31:41.920
I mean, I also have a question of my own,

01:31:41.920 --> 01:31:43.720
but I think, this is more important.

01:31:44.560 --> 01:31:45.650
So, if y'all could just

01:31:45.650 --> 01:31:47.450
kind of speak slowly when you answer,

01:31:47.450 --> 01:31:48.283
if we're gonna try

01:31:48.283 --> 01:31:49.820
to kind of write down your response,

01:31:49.820 --> 01:31:51.370
that would be really helpful.

01:31:51.370 --> 01:31:52.800
So, this is technically for Derecka.

01:31:52.800 --> 01:31:54.450
I think, it can work for anyone.

01:31:54.450 --> 01:31:57.150
It says, "Hello, Derecka Purnell,

01:31:57.150 --> 01:31:59.280
I'm a prisoner in Texas
and I received a copy

01:31:59.280 --> 01:32:01.420
of your book through No Name Books in L.A.

01:32:01.420 --> 01:32:02.930
I became politicized in 2017

01:32:02.930 --> 01:32:04.240
through contact with Black and Pink,

01:32:04.240 --> 01:32:05.870
who you mentioned in your book.

01:32:05.870 --> 01:32:06.980
It's been my observation

01:32:06.980 --> 01:32:08.650
that the abolition community is made up

01:32:08.650 --> 01:32:10.740
of a vast number of diverse collectives.

01:32:10.740 --> 01:32:12.750
And I was wondering if
you had any suggestions

01:32:12.750 --> 01:32:13.980
as to how these various collectives

01:32:13.980 --> 01:32:15.400
can communicate with each other,

01:32:15.400 --> 01:32:16.640
and become aware of each other,

01:32:16.640 --> 01:32:18.153
so we can organize as a whole.

01:32:19.440 --> 01:32:24.440
- That's so cool, that's a...

01:32:25.060 --> 01:32:27.290
I'm at least familiar with

01:32:27.290 --> 01:32:29.100
the collections and coalitions

01:32:29.100 --> 01:32:31.030
among abolitionist organizations

01:32:31.030 --> 01:32:33.550
is how actually, I came to learn

01:32:33.550 --> 01:32:35.480
about a lot of abolish organizations,

01:32:35.480 --> 01:32:39.360
because I'm a part of
groups and collectives,

01:32:39.360 --> 01:32:42.550
and convenings where people are trying

01:32:42.550 --> 01:32:44.750
to figure out how to align our work.

01:32:44.750 --> 01:32:47.446
And so, those aren't typically broadcast,

01:32:47.446 --> 01:32:50.050
or public, but they happen.

01:32:50.050 --> 01:32:51.490
They happen regularly.

01:32:51.490 --> 01:32:56.140
The last one I actually
organized was in 2000...

01:32:56.140 --> 01:32:57.730
2020 was the last thing

01:32:57.730 --> 01:32:59.320
I did before the pandemic.

01:32:59.320 --> 01:33:02.962
And it was a convening
on police abolition.

01:33:02.962 --> 01:33:03.795
And we had no idea

01:33:03.795 --> 01:33:05.900
that seven months later, defund the police

01:33:05.900 --> 01:33:09.240
would be the primary
demand from the streets.

01:33:09.240 --> 01:33:11.690
We just didn't know, 'cause this was,

01:33:11.690 --> 01:33:12.910
it was actually a sad weekend

01:33:12.910 --> 01:33:14.690
this weekend, Kobe Bryant died.

01:33:14.690 --> 01:33:16.230
That weekend was so transformational

01:33:16.230 --> 01:33:19.690
and so memorable for lots
of different reasons.

01:33:19.690 --> 01:33:22.480
But, in that convening,
there were maybe...

01:33:22.480 --> 01:33:24.800
There were dozens of organizations there,

01:33:24.800 --> 01:33:27.630
maybe 100 people who
were trying to figure out

01:33:27.630 --> 01:33:30.730
our lines of difference,
and how to build coalition.

01:33:30.730 --> 01:33:32.120
And I know critical resistances

01:33:32.120 --> 01:33:34.980
had conveniences of people
doing abolitionist work.

01:33:34.980 --> 01:33:36.770
MUMI was also incredible.

01:33:36.770 --> 01:33:41.770
One of the last abolitionist
convenings that I attended.

01:33:41.950 --> 01:33:44.000
I think that what's...

01:33:44.000 --> 01:33:46.210
That I don't know if the person

01:33:46.210 --> 01:33:48.380
who was asking that question is asking

01:33:48.380 --> 01:33:50.703
for something that feels
like a United front.

01:33:52.783 --> 01:33:55.220
Yeah, I want to say that, I think, any

01:33:55.220 --> 01:33:57.950
of those number of people who
I write about in the book,

01:33:57.950 --> 01:34:00.028
they know about the other
stuff that's going on.

01:34:00.028 --> 01:34:02.180
And so, I don't know if it means

01:34:02.180 --> 01:34:04.270
that it needs to be advertised better,

01:34:04.270 --> 01:34:06.500
but most of those convenings

01:34:06.500 --> 01:34:08.660
is except for the public one like you did,

01:34:08.660 --> 01:34:10.260
or strategy convenings.

01:34:10.260 --> 01:34:12.670
We're trying to figure out like, okay,

01:34:12.670 --> 01:34:14.120
when people ask, what about the murders,

01:34:14.120 --> 01:34:16.100
and what about sexual violence?

01:34:16.100 --> 01:34:18.210
What's the work that's
happening in communities

01:34:18.210 --> 01:34:20.880
to disrupt that, and
to help minimize that?

01:34:20.880 --> 01:34:23.140
What are you guys doing
in your neighborhood,

01:34:23.140 --> 01:34:24.870
how can we practice that
in our neighborhood?

01:34:24.870 --> 01:34:27.340
And so, that is happening.

01:34:27.340 --> 01:34:30.510
Fortunately, I would assume
that a lot of these people know.

01:34:30.510 --> 01:34:32.660
I think that the thing
I'm actually concerned

01:34:32.660 --> 01:34:37.237
about is whether the prison
police abolition folks

01:34:37.237 --> 01:34:40.720
are actually missing out
on the socialism stuff.

01:34:40.720 --> 01:34:43.410
There's like a... There's DSA over here

01:34:43.410 --> 01:34:45.277
and abolitionist organizations over there.

01:34:45.277 --> 01:34:47.632
And they're like sunrises in the middle.

01:34:47.632 --> 01:34:49.115
(audience chuckles)

01:34:49.115 --> 01:34:50.120
So, there's all these interesting,

01:34:50.120 --> 01:34:52.280
important organizations
that are coming up,

01:34:52.280 --> 01:34:55.410
and I'm nervous that their
politics are sort of insular

01:34:55.410 --> 01:34:58.500
and wrapped around their particular thing.

01:34:58.500 --> 01:35:01.240
And so, I've met, yeah,
I've met abolitionists.

01:35:01.240 --> 01:35:02.660
People who claim they're abolitionist

01:35:02.660 --> 01:35:04.540
and still hold onto capitalism.

01:35:04.540 --> 01:35:07.410
I've met socialists who are
staunch anti-abolitionists.

01:35:07.410 --> 01:35:10.680
So, trying to get people together,

01:35:10.680 --> 01:35:12.520
think through our class analyses

01:35:12.520 --> 01:35:14.430
and our colonial analyses is I think,

01:35:14.430 --> 01:35:16.570
that work has to do a lot more.

01:35:16.570 --> 01:35:17.690
I can give this to you

01:35:17.690 --> 01:35:20.033
so you don't have to try
to type it all right now,

01:35:20.033 --> 01:35:22.260
and then, I can also
make some recommendations

01:35:22.260 --> 01:35:24.542
on where that person can start.

01:35:24.542 --> 01:35:25.824
(audience chatters indistinctly)

01:35:25.824 --> 01:35:26.848
- [Garrett] Yeah, can I just add to?

01:35:26.848 --> 01:35:28.848
One, I think, we're gonna have

01:35:28.848 --> 01:35:31.547
a transcript of this, so we can also...

01:35:31.547 --> 01:35:32.782
That will be much easier.

01:35:32.782 --> 01:35:33.965
- [Elizabeth] And we're recording a video.

01:35:33.965 --> 01:35:38.180
- And to this question about, I mean,

01:35:38.180 --> 01:35:40.760
this is sort of like
a call to action again

01:35:40.760 --> 01:35:43.070
from the front, and some tangible ways

01:35:43.070 --> 01:35:43.903
to get involved.

01:35:43.903 --> 01:35:46.910
I think, so much of the work that has

01:35:46.910 --> 01:35:50.000
to be done across walls is between us

01:35:50.000 --> 01:35:51.440
and people inside.

01:35:51.440 --> 01:35:54.010
But then, there's this other barrier too,

01:35:54.010 --> 01:35:55.840
I think, collective
dreaming and organizing,

01:35:55.840 --> 01:35:58.790
which is people inside between, right?

01:35:58.790 --> 01:36:02.940
And like the gender segregation,
that happens in prisons.

01:36:02.940 --> 01:36:04.170
I mean, the organizing that happens

01:36:04.170 --> 01:36:06.970
in different prisons is different.

01:36:06.970 --> 01:36:09.438
And so, one of the things
that we've been trying to do

01:36:09.438 --> 01:36:11.410
with Study and Struggle is really act

01:36:11.410 --> 01:36:13.730
as facilitators outside so people inside

01:36:13.730 --> 01:36:16.060
can share organizing with each other.

01:36:16.060 --> 01:36:19.840
So, that's part of why
we produce transcripts

01:36:19.840 --> 01:36:21.540
and send them inside, and have panels

01:36:21.540 --> 01:36:23.560
with incarcerated people
from different states.

01:36:23.560 --> 01:36:24.820
And then, produce a transcript of that

01:36:24.820 --> 01:36:26.283
to send to other states.

01:36:27.540 --> 01:36:28.950
And this is not new work.

01:36:28.950 --> 01:36:30.780
I mean, I don't know if folks are familiar

01:36:30.780 --> 01:36:34.860
with Yuri Kochiyama, but I've been talking

01:36:34.860 --> 01:36:37.560
to political prisoners from that era.

01:36:37.560 --> 01:36:40.560
And they talk about writing people inside.

01:36:40.560 --> 01:36:42.410
And I was like, "Oh, and back in the day,

01:36:42.410 --> 01:36:43.500
you could write to people on the inside?"

01:36:43.500 --> 01:36:44.610
And they were like, "No, I wrote to Yuri,

01:36:44.610 --> 01:36:46.420
and then she sent..."

01:36:46.420 --> 01:36:49.240
So, there's these interlocutors outside

01:36:49.240 --> 01:36:50.073
and it's, I'm not...

01:36:50.073 --> 01:36:51.690
I mean, this is not sexy work.

01:36:51.690 --> 01:36:55.200
This is painstaking unseen labor,

01:36:55.200 --> 01:36:56.490
but to be the people

01:36:56.490 --> 01:36:59.790
who are facilitating those relationships,

01:36:59.790 --> 01:37:01.730
because then people inside don't have

01:37:01.730 --> 01:37:05.520
to constantly, in
isolation from one another,

01:37:05.520 --> 01:37:07.950
invent and reinvent these things.

01:37:07.950 --> 01:37:09.800
I just think so much of what

01:37:10.790 --> 01:37:13.840
the prison is doing is that isolation,

01:37:13.840 --> 01:37:16.080
and it's not just a geographic separation.

01:37:16.080 --> 01:37:18.183
It's a barrier to the collective knowledge

01:37:18.183 --> 01:37:19.680
that we all have, and did have.

01:37:19.680 --> 01:37:21.598
- Yeah, can I give one
quick example about that?

01:37:21.598 --> 01:37:22.722
- Yes.

01:37:22.722 --> 01:37:23.670
- Just to make it very, very clear,

01:37:23.670 --> 01:37:26.230
in many states, if not all states,

01:37:26.230 --> 01:37:27.830
if you try to start a petition,

01:37:27.830 --> 01:37:29.960
you can get into solitary confinement.

01:37:29.960 --> 01:37:31.970
And a petition is anything that has more

01:37:31.970 --> 01:37:32.978
than one signature.

01:37:32.978 --> 01:37:34.561
And so, there's like organizations

01:37:34.561 --> 01:37:37.660
like fight toxic prisons,
who work with people

01:37:37.660 --> 01:37:40.398
who are inside to do
one signature petitions,

01:37:40.398 --> 01:37:43.080
'cause that's how they
have to build momentum

01:37:43.080 --> 01:37:44.775
for their cause.

01:37:44.775 --> 01:37:48.853
When you talk about
fascism inside of prisons,

01:37:48.853 --> 01:37:52.150
if you try to do something as simple

01:37:52.150 --> 01:37:54.300
as get two, or three, 10 signatures,

01:37:54.300 --> 01:37:55.880
try to organize in the wing.

01:37:55.880 --> 01:37:56.970
You're trying to start it riot,

01:37:56.970 --> 01:37:57.850
you're trying to (mumbles indistinctly).

01:37:57.850 --> 01:37:59.090
So, this is the importance of,

01:37:59.090 --> 01:38:00.519
I just wanted to illustrate that,

01:38:00.519 --> 01:38:02.660
cause that to me is one of the examples

01:38:02.660 --> 01:38:04.350
that make it so bright.

01:38:04.350 --> 01:38:05.970
- And can I say one more thing?

01:38:05.970 --> 01:38:07.840
So much of the work we do outside

01:38:07.840 --> 01:38:10.060
is online exclusively.

01:38:10.060 --> 01:38:13.550
People inside have no
way of accessing that,

01:38:13.550 --> 01:38:14.720
which is obvious to say,

01:38:14.720 --> 01:38:16.870
but to the extent that you can create

01:38:16.870 --> 01:38:20.810
a sort of printable
version of that for people.

01:38:20.810 --> 01:38:23.407
Like I constantly just
find myself emailing people

01:38:23.407 --> 01:38:25.060
and like, "Was there a transcript?

01:38:25.060 --> 01:38:25.940
Is there a PDF?"

01:38:25.940 --> 01:38:27.520
I'm like copying and pasting websites

01:38:27.520 --> 01:38:29.080
into Word documents, and sending them.

01:38:29.080 --> 01:38:31.420
So, I just think, we need to be cognizant

01:38:31.420 --> 01:38:34.260
of how that is a barrier

01:38:34.260 --> 01:38:35.760
for people who are organizing.

01:38:37.120 --> 01:38:39.250
- Thank you so much for that.

01:38:39.250 --> 01:38:40.083
- Thank you.

01:38:40.083 --> 01:38:40.916
- Thank you for that question.

01:38:40.916 --> 01:38:41.749
- Do you wanna...

01:38:41.749 --> 01:38:42.719
Did you wanna ask your question, or...

01:38:42.719 --> 01:38:43.827
(crosstalk drowns out speaker)

01:38:43.827 --> 01:38:44.660
- Oh, okay.
- Okay.

01:38:44.660 --> 01:38:45.493
- Okay, all right, cool.

01:38:45.493 --> 01:38:46.326
Thanks (indistinct).

01:38:46.326 --> 01:38:47.440
Okay, my...

01:38:47.440 --> 01:38:48.810
Okay, this is on, right?

01:38:48.810 --> 01:38:50.320
Okay, yeah, cool, all right.

01:38:50.320 --> 01:38:51.410
Okay, my other question

01:38:51.410 --> 01:38:53.240
is that I think there's a lot

01:38:53.240 --> 01:38:55.180
of appreciable tension and movement work

01:38:55.180 --> 01:38:56.630
between legal and extra legal action.

01:38:56.630 --> 01:38:57.610
I guess, this is kind of pushing

01:38:57.610 --> 01:38:58.500
a little bit more on the question

01:38:58.500 --> 01:39:00.370
that you asked previously.

01:39:00.370 --> 01:39:01.580
I think, a lot of folks inside

01:39:01.580 --> 01:39:03.276
sometimes do emphasize
kind of legal action

01:39:03.276 --> 01:39:05.090
just for, I don't know, small things.

01:39:05.090 --> 01:39:06.860
Like I don't wanna cut my hair

01:39:06.860 --> 01:39:07.720
and things like that.

01:39:07.720 --> 01:39:09.710
In this vein, how would

01:39:09.710 --> 01:39:11.840
you define non-reformist reforms,

01:39:11.840 --> 01:39:13.190
and what place, if any, do you think

01:39:13.190 --> 01:39:14.613
they have in movement work?

01:39:18.650 --> 01:39:20.229
- You used the term you don't want to.

01:39:20.229 --> 01:39:23.573
- Mm-hmm, no, you're right
about this right now,

01:39:23.573 --> 01:39:24.998
so it's all good.

01:39:24.998 --> 01:39:26.226
- About non reformist reforms.

01:39:26.226 --> 01:39:27.227
- No, about-

01:39:27.227 --> 01:39:28.060
(crosstalk drowns out speaker)

01:39:28.060 --> 01:39:28.893
- About using that.

01:39:28.893 --> 01:39:30.451
- But yeah.
- Yeah.

01:39:30.451 --> 01:39:31.740
- (sighs) Okay, so I'll take...

01:39:31.740 --> 01:39:33.810
I mean, so I'm right, yeah.

01:39:33.810 --> 01:39:35.620
- Let me throw this question in there too-

01:39:35.620 --> 01:39:36.630
- Okay.
- From online, just 'cause

01:39:36.630 --> 01:39:38.574
we're running out of time.

01:39:38.574 --> 01:39:40.590
And I think, it's on a related point

01:39:40.590 --> 01:39:41.800
from that question and just

01:39:41.800 --> 01:39:43.103
the discussion in general.

01:39:44.130 --> 01:39:48.990
And it comes from someone Benehoff online,

01:39:48.990 --> 01:39:50.070
and it says, "Comrades inside

01:39:50.070 --> 01:39:51.470
are often struggling to survive

01:39:51.470 --> 01:39:53.420
the imminent threats and
slow death of prison.

01:39:53.420 --> 01:39:54.420
How do we bridge the gap

01:39:54.420 --> 01:39:56.770
between survival and politics?"

01:39:56.770 --> 01:39:58.100
And then, this is another one

01:39:58.100 --> 01:39:59.260
I'm just gonna throw out.

01:39:59.260 --> 01:40:00.769
Throwing a lot out there.

01:40:00.769 --> 01:40:01.602
Alternatively, would y'all mind speaking

01:40:01.602 --> 01:40:03.970
a bit about the dialectic
between the struggle

01:40:03.970 --> 01:40:06.500
to survive incarceration
and the development

01:40:06.500 --> 01:40:08.053
of the abolitionist movement?

01:40:09.458 --> 01:40:12.630
(audience laughs)

01:40:12.630 --> 01:40:14.009
- I see what you did there. (laughs)

01:40:14.009 --> 01:40:15.101
(Derecka laughs)

01:40:15.101 --> 01:40:16.730
Okay, I'll take a piece.

01:40:16.730 --> 01:40:18.890
- Okay.
- Okay, so...

01:40:18.890 --> 01:40:20.420
- There's a way you can weight it all.

01:40:20.420 --> 01:40:25.180
- Yeah, I mean, I think, this gets back

01:40:25.180 --> 01:40:27.890
to the question about law
and abolition in a way.

01:40:27.890 --> 01:40:30.450
And it's just that, so Derecka

01:40:30.450 --> 01:40:32.830
very carefully crafted the difference

01:40:32.830 --> 01:40:37.810
between the use of law,
and being a lawyer,

01:40:37.810 --> 01:40:38.643
and an abolitionist.

01:40:38.643 --> 01:40:43.530
Like a vocational sort
of careerist version,

01:40:43.530 --> 01:40:46.290
versus the tool of law.

01:40:46.290 --> 01:40:48.630
And I think, that goes for a lot

01:40:48.630 --> 01:40:49.463
of different things.

01:40:49.463 --> 01:40:51.020
Which is that I don't think we can afford

01:40:51.020 --> 01:40:54.240
to leave any tools on the table, right?

01:40:54.240 --> 01:40:58.820
These are all potentially subversive tools

01:40:58.820 --> 01:41:00.313
if used appropriately.

01:41:01.894 --> 01:41:02.727
And that doesn't mean

01:41:02.727 --> 01:41:03.970
that they're not dangerous tools

01:41:03.970 --> 01:41:07.810
that can wind up being reforms,

01:41:07.810 --> 01:41:10.350
and therefore, fascist.

01:41:10.350 --> 01:41:14.570
But yeah, I mean, I'm writing about

01:41:14.570 --> 01:41:16.270
a person who is a revolutionary anarchist,

01:41:16.270 --> 01:41:17.190
and a jailhouse lawyer,

01:41:17.190 --> 01:41:21.080
and used constitutional law to...

01:41:22.230 --> 01:41:24.940
To mitigate the circumstances
that he was organizing in.

01:41:24.940 --> 01:41:25.990
I mean, he was at one point

01:41:25.990 --> 01:41:29.250
in solitary confinement,
unable to communicate

01:41:29.250 --> 01:41:31.610
with a single person besides his lawyer.

01:41:31.610 --> 01:41:34.090
So, there, the law is both,

01:41:34.090 --> 01:41:37.720
the sort of only gateway
to the outside world,

01:41:37.720 --> 01:41:41.040
and he knew how to write a writ

01:41:41.040 --> 01:41:43.610
that wound up in Constance
Baker Motley's court

01:41:43.610 --> 01:41:44.840
and got him out of solitary.

01:41:44.840 --> 01:41:45.850
So, he could do other things

01:41:45.850 --> 01:41:47.520
like write a revolutionary newspaper,

01:41:47.520 --> 01:41:50.380
and organize a chapter of
the Black Panther Party.

01:41:50.380 --> 01:41:53.627
So, it's, I think, that
kind of relationship of,

01:41:53.627 --> 01:41:55.640
I mean he wasn't thinking

01:41:55.640 --> 01:41:58.090
like this constitution
that's so sacrosanct

01:41:58.090 --> 01:42:01.440
will abolish the state.

01:42:01.440 --> 01:42:05.300
But that was a... That was
basically the only tool available

01:42:05.300 --> 01:42:06.220
to him, really.

01:42:06.220 --> 01:42:08.850
So, I think, that's my
answer to that question.

01:42:12.080 --> 01:42:13.267
- Oh no, go ahead.

01:42:13.267 --> 01:42:14.100
- No, you can go, so go ahead.

01:42:14.100 --> 01:42:15.550
- You know more than me.

01:42:15.550 --> 01:42:17.870
Well, I'm only gonna say one thing

01:42:17.870 --> 01:42:18.790
about the law,
(group chuckles)

01:42:18.790 --> 01:42:20.210
which may not answer the question,

01:42:20.210 --> 01:42:23.930
but just because I've been...

01:42:23.930 --> 01:42:26.680
I'm writing something on
Mutulu Shakur right now,

01:42:26.680 --> 01:42:29.730
and trying desperately to get him out.

01:42:29.730 --> 01:42:32.250
And Mutulu has been in
prison for a long time,

01:42:32.250 --> 01:42:37.250
precisely because when
he got law up in 1986,

01:42:38.010 --> 01:42:42.923
he and others made the case
that they are prisoners of war.

01:42:43.880 --> 01:42:45.700
In other words, they turned
to international law,

01:42:45.700 --> 01:42:48.250
international courts
and Geneva Conventions.

01:42:48.250 --> 01:42:50.610
And that's, so the federal courts,

01:42:50.610 --> 01:42:52.820
they hate that, the judge hates that.

01:42:52.820 --> 01:42:57.820
And so his mandatory parole had passed,

01:43:00.350 --> 01:43:03.480
and he's still locked up,
had COVID, still locked up.

01:43:03.480 --> 01:43:04.810
So, I think, that there's something

01:43:04.810 --> 01:43:07.540
to say just for those of
you who are interested,

01:43:07.540 --> 01:43:11.490
to look at international law,
to look at that as an option.

01:43:11.490 --> 01:43:12.480
To look at what it means

01:43:12.480 --> 01:43:15.750
to be a prisoner of war, trying to fight

01:43:15.750 --> 01:43:17.313
for justice and that
there's certain protections

01:43:17.313 --> 01:43:19.513
that prisoners of war
are supposed to have,

01:43:20.480 --> 01:43:21.910
in the United States, or elsewhere.

01:43:21.910 --> 01:43:24.310
So, that's just one area of the law

01:43:24.310 --> 01:43:26.220
we don't always think about,

01:43:26.220 --> 01:43:28.950
that's not necessarily reformist reform,

01:43:28.950 --> 01:43:31.663
but Derecka knows more than me.

01:43:32.875 --> 01:43:36.193
- (chuckles) What was the part
of the last question online?

01:43:37.130 --> 01:43:38.310
Bridge the gap.
- About survival.

01:43:38.310 --> 01:43:39.560
- [Elizabeth] Yeah, how
do we bridge the gap

01:43:39.560 --> 01:43:41.180
between survival and politics,

01:43:41.180 --> 01:43:44.026
and yeah, we'll just say that.

01:43:44.026 --> 01:43:45.020
- But wait, no, what was the second part,

01:43:45.020 --> 01:43:45.926
'cause that was the part-

01:43:45.926 --> 01:43:47.810
- The second part was,
"Would y'all mind speaking

01:43:47.810 --> 01:43:49.490
a bit about the dialectic
between the struggle

01:43:49.490 --> 01:43:51.350
to survive incarceration,
and the development

01:43:51.350 --> 01:43:53.027
of the abolitionist movement?"

01:43:53.950 --> 01:43:55.500
- Yeah, so I was...

01:43:55.500 --> 01:43:58.240
A similar point came up earlier today

01:43:58.240 --> 01:44:00.590
in the conversation I was
having about abolition,

01:44:00.590 --> 01:44:03.690
and the way that the
person sort of framed it,

01:44:03.690 --> 01:44:06.120
it was as if the abolitionists

01:44:06.120 --> 01:44:09.219
are outside talking about abolitionist,

01:44:09.219 --> 01:44:11.158
doing abolitionist organizing.

01:44:11.158 --> 01:44:12.770
And there are people who
are incarcerated dealing

01:44:12.770 --> 01:44:15.620
with the real realities of the prison.

01:44:15.620 --> 01:44:20.490
And they don't have time for
the abolitionist stuff outside,

01:44:20.490 --> 01:44:22.900
because they need access to food.

01:44:22.900 --> 01:44:24.220
They need access to water.

01:44:24.220 --> 01:44:27.820
All of this, they experience overcrowding.

01:44:27.820 --> 01:44:29.000
And what I...

01:44:30.130 --> 01:44:30.990
How can I say this?

01:44:30.990 --> 01:44:32.569
The bridge is there,

01:44:32.569 --> 01:44:36.600
the work comes from that bridge, right?

01:44:36.600 --> 01:44:40.010
Because most of the people,
at least the tradition,

01:44:40.010 --> 01:44:41.600
I hope that I belong to that.

01:44:41.600 --> 01:44:43.560
I see myself belong to the...

01:44:43.560 --> 01:44:48.510
For 40 years, at least, of
riotings and organizing around

01:44:48.510 --> 01:44:50.160
the prison industrial complex.

01:44:50.160 --> 01:44:51.860
So much of that direction has come

01:44:51.860 --> 01:44:53.030
from the people inside.

01:44:53.030 --> 01:44:56.002
It's like... But for their organizing,

01:44:56.002 --> 01:44:58.650
their rebellions, their uprisings,

01:44:58.650 --> 01:45:00.550
I think, that has given rise

01:45:00.550 --> 01:45:02.610
to the prison abolition movement outside

01:45:02.610 --> 01:45:03.547
of prisons, right?

01:45:03.547 --> 01:45:06.676
And so, you're talking about
Angela Davis speaking in 1994,

01:45:06.676 --> 01:45:09.540
and speaking from someone

01:45:09.540 --> 01:45:11.627
who was once a political prisoner, right?

01:45:11.627 --> 01:45:13.910
And so, that it's...

01:45:13.910 --> 01:45:17.110
It's not like how do we get
this to reach each other,

01:45:17.110 --> 01:45:21.950
but so much of that survivor
work, that risk taking,

01:45:21.950 --> 01:45:25.090
that then informs the struggle
that takes place outside.

01:45:25.090 --> 01:45:29.340
And I wanna emphasize
that, so, so, so much,

01:45:29.340 --> 01:45:31.810
'cause we're in an era
where there's this thing

01:45:31.810 --> 01:45:33.470
around deference politics.

01:45:33.470 --> 01:45:36.350
Like you defer to people who
are directly impacted, right?

01:45:36.350 --> 01:45:37.790
But the people who they want you

01:45:37.790 --> 01:45:39.830
to defer to, who are directly impacted

01:45:39.830 --> 01:45:41.590
are people who they decide.

01:45:41.590 --> 01:45:43.260
And usually, people who want to maintain

01:45:43.260 --> 01:45:44.972
the power of prisons.
- Mm-hmm.

01:45:44.972 --> 01:45:48.508
- Right, I've never seen at
philanthropy conferences,

01:45:48.508 --> 01:45:52.870
abolition has directly
impacted people, or survivors.

01:45:52.870 --> 01:45:55.080
The type of survivors that people

01:45:55.080 --> 01:45:56.870
in the mainstream choose to uplift.

01:45:56.870 --> 01:45:59.450
We need to be listening to
these kinds of black women,

01:45:59.450 --> 01:46:01.980
but they're not black women
abolitionist survivors, right?

01:46:01.980 --> 01:46:04.600
And so, I think, what's important for us

01:46:04.600 --> 01:46:06.980
to always name and to explain is that

01:46:06.980 --> 01:46:09.170
so much of our grassroot organizing,

01:46:09.170 --> 01:46:12.050
so much of what makes it
into the halls at Harvard,

01:46:12.050 --> 01:46:14.230
and to Yale has first been theorized

01:46:14.230 --> 01:46:17.203
and organized around people
who experienced the oppression.

01:46:17.203 --> 01:46:18.170
- Right.

01:46:18.170 --> 01:46:20.210
- That that is true of radical politics.

01:46:20.210 --> 01:46:22.440
That is very, very, very, very true,

01:46:22.440 --> 01:46:24.017
and we must not forget that.

01:46:24.017 --> 01:46:26.570
And so, when I hear
critiques of abolition being

01:46:26.570 --> 01:46:28.177
an academic exercise, I'm like,

01:46:28.177 --> 01:46:30.150
"Oh wait, wait, wait, wait, wait,

01:46:30.150 --> 01:46:33.877
let's go back 40 years ago
before the uprising in 2020."

01:46:33.877 --> 01:46:37.140
Let's read the works of people,
let's look at the demands

01:46:37.140 --> 01:46:39.184
of prisoners doing these rebellions.

01:46:39.184 --> 01:46:40.017
- Mm-hmm.

01:46:40.017 --> 01:46:43.280
- Yes, they were asking for more freedoms,

01:46:43.280 --> 01:46:45.600
they were also asking to be free, right?

01:46:45.600 --> 01:46:47.190
They were asking for better food

01:46:47.190 --> 01:46:48.330
and a right to healthcare,

01:46:48.330 --> 01:46:49.163
'cause they didn't wanna die

01:46:49.163 --> 01:46:51.140
and suffer until they're released.

01:46:51.140 --> 01:46:53.680
Right, but they also
demanded to close prisons.

01:46:53.680 --> 01:46:57.080
Nas didn't say, if I ruled the world,

01:46:57.080 --> 01:46:59.479
I would free all nonviolent offenders.

01:46:59.479 --> 01:47:00.312
(group laughs and claps)

01:47:00.312 --> 01:47:02.153
Not in my version.

01:47:02.153 --> 01:47:03.302
Did you hear that?

01:47:03.302 --> 01:47:04.160
I didn't, did Lauren seen that on her...

01:47:04.160 --> 01:47:05.118
I didn't hear that version-

01:47:05.118 --> 01:47:06.488
- That's probably the clean version.

01:47:06.488 --> 01:47:08.420
- He said, "I free all my sons, all, all."

01:47:08.420 --> 01:47:09.270
- Mm-hmm.

01:47:09.270 --> 01:47:11.450
- Take 'em outta Atticus,
send them to Africa,

01:47:11.450 --> 01:47:13.270
that's the version I heard.

01:47:13.270 --> 01:47:15.830
So, it's like, it's so much.

01:47:15.830 --> 01:47:17.400
You asked about culture earlier.

01:47:17.400 --> 01:47:19.270
It's so much in our culture, 'cause again,

01:47:19.270 --> 01:47:22.250
we learned that based on our experiences.

01:47:22.250 --> 01:47:23.780
And so, it doesn't make us experts,

01:47:23.780 --> 01:47:25.030
hopefully just helps keep

01:47:25.030 --> 01:47:27.230
us within that tradition.

01:47:27.230 --> 01:47:28.860
- Yeah, yeah, and by the way,

01:47:28.860 --> 01:47:30.550
the anti-Klan movement started in prison,

01:47:30.550 --> 01:47:31.738
you know that right?

01:47:31.738 --> 01:47:36.320
The anti-Klan movement
in 1980s began in prison

01:47:36.320 --> 01:47:38.580
with prisoners, who
were fighting the Klan,

01:47:38.580 --> 01:47:42.360
who were hired as guards and wardens.

01:47:42.360 --> 01:47:43.510
And we forget that.

01:47:43.510 --> 01:47:45.940
We think that somehow all these movements

01:47:45.940 --> 01:47:48.840
are on the outside in
that on the inside people

01:47:48.840 --> 01:47:50.190
are not fighting those same fights.

01:47:50.190 --> 01:47:51.603
This is a perfect example.

01:47:53.446 --> 01:47:54.576
(crosstalk drowns out speaker)

01:47:54.576 --> 01:47:56.076
- Go for it, yeah.

01:47:57.690 --> 01:48:00.030
- [Male] Hello, thank you so much

01:48:00.030 --> 01:48:03.419
for all being here.

01:48:03.419 --> 01:48:06.160
When I heard that there was a panel

01:48:06.160 --> 01:48:07.550
with all of you on it, I was like,

01:48:07.550 --> 01:48:08.989
how did you (laughs) get this many

01:48:08.989 --> 01:48:11.190
of the people who I admire,

01:48:11.190 --> 01:48:13.816
and that their fields of scholarship one

01:48:13.816 --> 01:48:15.243
to one, (chuckles) one desk.

01:48:16.530 --> 01:48:19.610
But I was really interested actually,

01:48:19.610 --> 01:48:21.170
and I feel like it's been a theme

01:48:21.170 --> 01:48:23.014
throughout everything
that you all have said.

01:48:23.014 --> 01:48:26.880
At the beginning, both,
sorry what was the name

01:48:26.880 --> 01:48:30.770
of the first of the two
people inside that you...

01:48:30.770 --> 01:48:31.671
Was it...

01:48:31.671 --> 01:48:32.603
- Oh, Lawrence.

01:48:32.603 --> 01:48:33.436
- Lawrence.
- Yeah.

01:48:33.436 --> 01:48:34.269
- Where sort of, Lawrence was saying,

01:48:34.269 --> 01:48:36.450
look mutual aid is great and all,

01:48:36.450 --> 01:48:38.760
but we're not...

01:48:38.760 --> 01:48:40.240
We also have to keep in mind

01:48:40.240 --> 01:48:43.163
sort of fighting to actually
end these structures.

01:48:44.820 --> 01:48:49.810
And I feel like there's
a disconnect that I see

01:48:49.810 --> 01:48:52.760
because I grew up fairly politicized,

01:48:52.760 --> 01:48:56.270
but I became an organizer
during the rebellions

01:48:56.270 --> 01:49:01.270
around Mike Brown and
Eric Garner in New York.

01:49:01.320 --> 01:49:04.100
And I feel like ever since that moment,

01:49:04.100 --> 01:49:05.170
I've just seen a disconnect

01:49:05.170 --> 01:49:09.444
between both what happens
in organizing spaces,

01:49:09.444 --> 01:49:12.400
and in movement spaces of protest.

01:49:12.400 --> 01:49:14.212
And then, an even bigger disconnect

01:49:14.212 --> 01:49:16.884
between what happens in these spaces,

01:49:16.884 --> 01:49:19.830
and what's happening in the streets.

01:49:19.830 --> 01:49:24.610
And I feel like it's so poignant

01:49:24.610 --> 01:49:25.986
in so many places.

01:49:25.986 --> 01:49:27.730
I don't wanna go into to details,

01:49:27.730 --> 01:49:28.950
but I've seen people

01:49:28.950 --> 01:49:31.590
who call themselves organizers, fighting

01:49:31.590 --> 01:49:33.706
to control the sort of,

01:49:33.706 --> 01:49:36.677
in their eyes, destructive like (mumbles),

01:49:38.711 --> 01:49:40.120
like destructive impulses

01:49:40.120 --> 01:49:42.380
of people who are just like, okay,

01:49:42.380 --> 01:49:45.240
these tops are screwing with us every day.

01:49:45.240 --> 01:49:46.320
I have friends inside.

01:49:46.320 --> 01:49:48.537
I want to burn this police
station to the ground.

01:49:48.537 --> 01:49:50.240
And they're like, "No, don't do that,

01:49:50.240 --> 01:49:51.470
we're nonviolent," right?

01:49:51.470 --> 01:49:53.093
And obviously like,
this is an exaggeration

01:49:53.093 --> 01:49:56.740
of what some people are on.

01:49:56.740 --> 01:49:58.993
And then you get to here, and I think,

01:49:59.852 --> 01:50:01.250
you think about the difference

01:50:01.250 --> 01:50:05.230
between the uprisings
at Mizu that happened

01:50:05.230 --> 01:50:06.670
in 2015, and what happened here.

01:50:06.670 --> 01:50:08.380
It was like, at Mizu, the first thing

01:50:08.380 --> 01:50:10.580
they were talking about
was their connections

01:50:10.580 --> 01:50:11.800
to Ferguson, 'cause it was so close.

01:50:11.800 --> 01:50:14.540
And here, you try to
mention police brutality

01:50:14.540 --> 01:50:15.373
and everyone was like,

01:50:15.373 --> 01:50:17.080
you're gonna destroy
our movement. (chuckles)

01:50:17.080 --> 01:50:19.220
And like, muddying the waters.

01:50:19.220 --> 01:50:22.190
And so, I guess, in terms of both of these

01:50:22.190 --> 01:50:24.560
kinds of disconnects, I do think

01:50:24.560 --> 01:50:26.170
that there is a really important role

01:50:26.170 --> 01:50:28.100
of those of us, who maybe have put

01:50:28.100 --> 01:50:31.492
a lot of premium on...

01:50:31.492 --> 01:50:32.960
Who have existed in a lot

01:50:32.960 --> 01:50:37.090
of these spaces in movements.

01:50:37.090 --> 01:50:40.690
But I guess, I wonder how do you build

01:50:40.690 --> 01:50:43.470
those kinds of long term,

01:50:43.470 --> 01:50:46.350
sort of movement to destroy these systems

01:50:46.350 --> 01:50:48.290
that you're talking about?

01:50:48.290 --> 01:50:50.860
How do you, sort of bridge those gaps?

01:50:50.860 --> 01:50:52.690
Because what you were saying earlier

01:50:52.690 --> 01:50:53.523
about like, okay,

01:50:53.523 --> 01:50:55.030
you have 12 million
people out on the streets,

01:50:55.030 --> 01:50:55.863
but you don't see them in front

01:50:55.863 --> 01:50:56.930
of the White House.

01:50:56.930 --> 01:50:58.490
I think that the flip side of that right,

01:50:58.490 --> 01:51:00.890
is that everybody is like, "Oh, well,

01:51:00.890 --> 01:51:02.540
what you're doing is just spontaneous,

01:51:02.540 --> 01:51:03.373
you gotta get organized."

01:51:03.373 --> 01:51:04.530
And then, people are
like, "Okay, so what do

01:51:04.530 --> 01:51:05.363
I do to get organized?"

01:51:05.363 --> 01:51:07.041
And they're like,
"Follow us on Instagram."

01:51:07.041 --> 01:51:08.060
(audience chuckles)

01:51:08.060 --> 01:51:09.810
Come out to campaign for this politician.

01:51:09.810 --> 01:51:11.040
And they're like, "Well,
that's not what we want.

01:51:11.040 --> 01:51:12.423
We were here to destroy the police."

01:51:12.423 --> 01:51:16.170
And so, I guess, obviously,
I know that a lot...

01:51:16.170 --> 01:51:17.160
Not to say this radical work

01:51:17.160 --> 01:51:18.535
isn't happening, and I'm not asking for...

01:51:18.535 --> 01:51:20.110
I'm sure there are plenty of people

01:51:20.110 --> 01:51:21.087
in this room who know
about some of this work.

01:51:21.087 --> 01:51:23.933
And I'm not asking to
Henry Box Brown anyone,

01:51:24.870 --> 01:51:26.320
but I guess, if you have some

01:51:26.320 --> 01:51:29.500
sort of, in the perhaps,
slightly more abstract,

01:51:29.500 --> 01:51:31.560
how to actually create the culture

01:51:31.560 --> 01:51:36.035
of sort of destruction
of policing and prisons,

01:51:36.035 --> 01:51:38.490
and take those cues from inside

01:51:38.490 --> 01:51:41.233
where people would say,
abolition, nothing less.

01:51:42.500 --> 01:51:45.400
- And you have one minute.

01:51:45.400 --> 01:51:46.866
- Sorry.
- To answer those,

01:51:46.866 --> 01:51:48.594
to answer that big question.

01:51:48.594 --> 01:51:49.560
- One minute.

01:51:49.560 --> 01:51:52.270
- One minute.
- One minute.

01:51:52.270 --> 01:51:53.140
- I'm not gonna answer the question,

01:51:53.140 --> 01:51:53.973
but I will say that everything

01:51:53.973 --> 01:51:57.441
that she just described is old.

01:51:57.441 --> 01:51:58.299
(group laughs)

01:51:58.299 --> 01:52:00.370
No, no I'm saying it's not new.

01:52:00.370 --> 01:52:01.948
That's not a critique.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:52:01.948 --> 01:52:02.923
- Yes.

01:52:02.923 --> 01:52:04.574
- What I'm saying is that this has been,

01:52:04.574 --> 01:52:05.810
you go back to mass mobilizations,

01:52:05.810 --> 01:52:08.530
versus what SNCC is doing in Mississippi.

01:52:08.530 --> 01:52:09.415
- Mm-hmm.

01:52:09.415 --> 01:52:10.248
- You count the number of people

01:52:10.248 --> 01:52:12.420
who are organizing the SNCC is very small.

01:52:12.420 --> 01:52:15.560
You count the people who
show up in Washington DC

01:52:15.560 --> 01:52:18.550
on August 28th, it's large.

01:52:18.550 --> 01:52:20.560
You think about all the divisions,

01:52:20.560 --> 01:52:23.750
sometimes with divisions
around social economic issues.

01:52:23.750 --> 01:52:26.540
Sometimes divisions around anti communism.

01:52:26.540 --> 01:52:29.200
So, it's like the difference of course,

01:52:29.200 --> 01:52:31.690
is social media and other strategies,

01:52:31.690 --> 01:52:33.330
but I'm saying, this is the age old issue.

01:52:33.330 --> 01:52:35.530
And I'm sure that both Garrett and Derecka

01:52:35.530 --> 01:52:37.918
have some solutions to that age old issue.

01:52:37.918 --> 01:52:41.340
(group laughs)

01:52:41.340 --> 01:52:43.030
- I'll just say no, I'll just say briefly,

01:52:43.030 --> 01:52:44.833
I do think that to say kind of the point

01:52:44.833 --> 01:52:47.250
that Derecka shared earlier about

01:52:47.250 --> 01:52:52.020
the disagreement, or the sort of, lack

01:52:52.020 --> 01:52:55.304
of clarity around what
we mean by abolition.

01:52:55.304 --> 01:52:58.170
We do want a mass movement,

01:52:58.170 --> 01:53:00.420
but I think, we do need to clarify.

01:53:00.420 --> 01:53:02.620
I mean, that's why I really take

01:53:02.620 --> 01:53:04.990
the task of defining abolition seriously

01:53:04.990 --> 01:53:06.260
as a worthy...

01:53:06.260 --> 01:53:11.260
'Cause I think, are we talking
about abolishing the state?

01:53:11.880 --> 01:53:13.470
And a lot of people are not talking

01:53:13.470 --> 01:53:17.150
about abolishing the state,
but then, how do we imagine

01:53:17.150 --> 01:53:19.840
a state existing without
police and prisons?

01:53:19.840 --> 01:53:21.330
I don't think that could exist,

01:53:21.330 --> 01:53:22.500
but people clearly do, 'cause they're not-

01:53:22.500 --> 01:53:24.131
- Oh, Garrett's an anarchist, this is new,

01:53:24.131 --> 01:53:26.496
I didn't know this.
(audience laughs)

01:53:26.496 --> 01:53:28.545
(speaker drowned out by laughter)

01:53:28.545 --> 01:53:30.528
This is spicy, I wasn't ready. (laughs)

01:53:30.528 --> 01:53:32.920
(group laughs)

01:53:32.920 --> 01:53:36.240
- Anyway, no so I just think,

01:53:36.240 --> 01:53:38.760
we need more space to really hash out

01:53:38.760 --> 01:53:39.772
what we're talking about.

01:53:39.772 --> 01:53:44.772
Are we talking about
abolition and capitalism?

01:53:44.840 --> 01:53:45.790
I didn't know what we were talking

01:53:45.790 --> 01:53:49.220
about that, so but no, I don't think

01:53:49.220 --> 01:53:51.060
it's a thing to be resolved

01:53:51.060 --> 01:53:53.323
as much like a contradiction to be lived.

01:53:54.269 --> 01:53:55.420
- Right, Derecka, you have the last word.

01:53:55.420 --> 01:53:57.020
- Last word, ooh, Jesus.

01:53:57.020 --> 01:54:01.390
Okay, so I agree with a big
part of your assessment.

01:54:01.390 --> 01:54:03.370
I wrote about this, actually in January

01:54:03.370 --> 01:54:06.255
that I'm nervous that
with rise of social media,

01:54:06.255 --> 01:54:09.163
what has happened with online organizing

01:54:09.163 --> 01:54:11.900
is that we have organizers,

01:54:11.900 --> 01:54:13.700
perhaps inadvertently have created

01:54:13.700 --> 01:54:15.320
a like, share, subscribe culture.

01:54:15.320 --> 01:54:16.730
And that's how people can get involved

01:54:16.730 --> 01:54:18.990
in the movement, which
is very, very different

01:54:18.990 --> 01:54:20.730
than come to this meeting, sit here

01:54:20.730 --> 01:54:23.370
for three hours to do an orientation.

01:54:23.370 --> 01:54:25.148
Can you offer this as a sacrifice

01:54:25.148 --> 01:54:27.660
of your time, and your
labor, and of your talent.

01:54:27.660 --> 01:54:30.130
So, I think, that organizers are trying

01:54:30.130 --> 01:54:32.692
to figure out how to do mass absorption

01:54:32.692 --> 01:54:35.140
with the rise of the internet.

01:54:35.140 --> 01:54:36.500
And I think, it's very challenging,

01:54:36.500 --> 01:54:39.220
and I've seen, and constantly working

01:54:39.220 --> 01:54:40.730
through those challenges.

01:54:40.730 --> 01:54:42.452
I think, it's similar also to

01:54:42.452 --> 01:54:44.881
what I was saying earlier
about people channeling

01:54:44.881 --> 01:54:48.330
our hopes and dreams

01:54:48.330 --> 01:54:50.680
into specific career
ambitions on the left.

01:54:50.680 --> 01:54:51.520
- Uh-huh.
- I'm very nervous about that.

01:54:51.520 --> 01:54:52.910
I think, a similar thing

01:54:52.910 --> 01:54:55.460
has happened too, around
how do we effectuate change?

01:54:55.460 --> 01:54:57.720
And so, in the long time ago,

01:54:57.720 --> 01:55:00.560
you would see these vibrant movements

01:55:00.560 --> 01:55:02.010
to shut things down.

01:55:02.010 --> 01:55:04.510
Like use all tactics for
revolutionary violence

01:55:04.510 --> 01:55:06.290
to the law to figure it out.

01:55:06.290 --> 01:55:08.000
Now, when something happens,

01:55:08.000 --> 01:55:10.170
everyone gives money to the ACLU.

01:55:10.170 --> 01:55:11.003
- Right.

01:55:11.003 --> 01:55:13.780
- hen have the ACOU become the frontline?

01:55:13.780 --> 01:55:17.541
No shade to the ACLU, not
really like no shade to ACLU.

01:55:17.541 --> 01:55:20.680
(group laughs)
But they become like,

01:55:20.680 --> 01:55:23.768
I don't know, the Che
Guevara, like (mumbles)...

01:55:23.768 --> 01:55:24.813
(group laughs)

01:55:24.813 --> 01:55:25.646
It's so wild.

01:55:25.646 --> 01:55:27.545
I'm like, every time there's a crisis,

01:55:27.545 --> 01:55:29.030
we're gonna take them to court.

01:55:29.030 --> 01:55:30.050
And then, people give money

01:55:30.050 --> 01:55:31.474
for them to go to court.

01:55:31.474 --> 01:55:33.270
And it's like, yeah,
we need to go to court,

01:55:33.270 --> 01:55:37.180
but we also need much
more mass mobilizations

01:55:37.180 --> 01:55:39.030
and people entering to organizations

01:55:39.030 --> 01:55:40.560
and finding political homes,

01:55:40.560 --> 01:55:42.420
to figure out how to
answer these questions.

01:55:42.420 --> 01:55:44.128
And so, I'm attracted to organizations

01:55:44.128 --> 01:55:46.750
like the centers, who is trying to build

01:55:46.750 --> 01:55:49.870
a youth movement to end militarism,

01:55:49.870 --> 01:55:52.450
who's fighting to keep
ROTCs off of campuses.

01:55:52.450 --> 01:55:54.485
- Yeah.
- Who's fighting against

01:55:54.485 --> 01:55:55.330
the 1030 peace transfer programs,

01:55:55.330 --> 01:55:57.250
who's trying to do deep
political organizing

01:55:57.250 --> 01:55:58.140
around these issues.

01:55:58.140 --> 01:56:02.310
And so, we have those examples that exist,

01:56:02.310 --> 01:56:04.210
and that are engaging in
lots of different tactics,

01:56:04.210 --> 01:56:06.230
and trying to figure out
how to absorb people,

01:56:06.230 --> 01:56:07.940
to then effectuate that, right?

01:56:07.940 --> 01:56:09.880
And so, I think the more we realize

01:56:09.880 --> 01:56:11.480
the terrain where people now

01:56:11.480 --> 01:56:15.070
are just donated to a
nonprofit, or people in the past

01:56:15.070 --> 01:56:16.720
who didn't have political representation

01:56:16.720 --> 01:56:17.930
that look like that.

01:56:17.930 --> 01:56:19.590
So, now, you think you're fighting

01:56:19.590 --> 01:56:20.980
a white supremacist structure,

01:56:20.980 --> 01:56:23.340
that's keeping black people, or women out,

01:56:23.340 --> 01:56:25.520
and now, we have more, the most amount

01:56:25.520 --> 01:56:27.280
of diverse representation.

01:56:27.280 --> 01:56:28.700
Three people are still being killed today

01:56:28.700 --> 01:56:29.570
by the police.

01:56:29.570 --> 01:56:32.260
More than a million black
people are still locked up.

01:56:32.260 --> 01:56:35.199
Black women still have wealth of $5, like-

01:56:35.199 --> 01:56:36.032
- Right.

01:56:36.032 --> 01:56:37.270
- So, the political representation

01:56:37.270 --> 01:56:40.230
through diversity is an
answer because who was it?

01:56:40.230 --> 01:56:41.347
Like Fred Hampton said,

01:56:41.347 --> 01:56:43.704
"We were saying white when we should

01:56:43.704 --> 01:56:44.537
have been saying capitalist, all right?

01:56:44.537 --> 01:56:45.620
We were our critiques for it,

01:56:45.620 --> 01:56:47.370
like being lost in the wrong places.

01:56:47.370 --> 01:56:50.870
And so, I think that we
absolutely need political homes.

01:56:50.870 --> 01:56:53.470
We need organizations to really
think about the traditions

01:56:53.470 --> 01:56:56.160
that get people off of
line and into seats.

01:56:56.160 --> 01:56:57.583
And that's very hard, but it's something

01:56:57.583 --> 01:57:00.143
that we just have to figure out and do.

01:57:00.143 --> 01:57:01.300
- Mm-hmm.
(group applauds)

01:57:01.300 --> 01:57:02.743
- Please join me...

01:57:02.743 --> 01:57:06.161
(audience cheers and applauds)

01:57:06.161 --> 01:57:08.400
Thank you all for just an incredible

01:57:08.400 --> 01:57:09.603
and eye opening discussion.
- Yeah, thank you.

01:57:09.603 --> 01:57:13.103
Thank you, Liz. Thank you so, so, so much.

